PathwayData.put("data/grammar_vocabulary/units/L05U5.json",{"strand":"grammar_vocabulary","level":5,"cefr":"B1a","unit":5,"theme":"Sport and Challenge","essential_question":"What helps us keep going when things get difficult?","lessons":[{"id":"GV-L05-U5-LS1","title":"He Managed to Finish! could, was able to, managed to","objectives":["Students will be able to use could and couldn't for general ability in the past.","Students will be able to use was able to and managed to for success on a specific occasion, and will be able to for the future.","I can say \"I could swim when I was six.\"","I can say \"In the end, I managed to finish the race.\""],"objectives_ar":["سيتمكن الطلاب من استخدام could وcouldn't للقدرة العامة في الماضي.","سيتمكن الطلاب من استخدام was able to وmanaged to للنجاح في موقف محدد، وwill be able to للمستقبل.","أستطيع أن أقول I could swim when I was six.","أستطيع أن أقول In the end, I managed to finish the race."],"vocabulary":[{"word":"athlete","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈæθˌlit/","definition":"a person who is good at sports and trains to compete","arabic":"رياضي","example":"The athlete trains six days a week.","image_id":"GV-L05-U5-LS1-W01","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L05-U5-LS1-W01"},{"word":"coach","pos":"noun","ipa":"/koʊtʃ/","definition":"a person who trains a sports team or player","arabic":"مدرّب","example":"Our coach gives us great advice.","image_id":"GV-L05-U5-LS1-W02","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L05-U5-LS1-W02"},{"word":"opponent","pos":"noun","ipa":"/əˈpoʊnənt/","definition":"the person or team you play against","arabic":"خصم / منافس","example":"Our opponents were very strong.","image_id":"GV-L05-U5-LS1-W03","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L05-U5-LS1-W03"},{"word":"tournament","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈtʊrnəmənt/","definition":"a competition with many games or matches","arabic":"بطولة","example":"Our school won the football tournament.","image_id":"GV-L05-U5-LS1-W04","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L05-U5-LS1-W04"}],"explanation":{"text":"Use could / couldn't + base verb for general ability in the past – something you could do at any time: When I was six, I could swim. My grandpa could ride a horse.\nFor success on one specific occasion, use was/were able to or managed to – NOT could: In the end, he was able to finish the race. She managed to score a goal in the last minute. (managed to shows it was difficult.)\nIn the negative, couldn't is fine for both: He couldn't run fast after the injury. can has no future form, so use will be able to: Next year, I'll be able to win.\ngeneral past ability → could / couldn't + base verb\none occasion, success → was/were able to / managed to + base verb\nnegative (general or one occasion) → couldn't\nfuture → will be able to (NOT will can)","examples":["My grandfather could ride a camel when he was five.","I couldn't swim until I was ten.","Despite the rain, we were able to finish the match.","Lina managed to solve the hardest puzzle.","The goalkeeper couldn't stop the penalty.","After months of training, he was able to run ten kilometers.","Will you be able to come to the tournament next week?","The firefighters managed to save everyone."],"common_errors":["✗ Yesterday he could win the match. → ✓ Yesterday he was able to win / managed to win the match. (One specific success → was able to / managed to.)","✗ I will can swim next summer. → ✓ I will be able to swim next summer. (Future → will be able to.)","✗ She managed finish the race. → ✓ She managed to finish the race. (managed to + base verb.)","✗ He could to ride a bike at five. → ✓ He could ride a bike at five. (could + base verb (no to).)"],"arabic":"could + المصدر للقدرة العامة في الماضي: When I was six, I could swim.\nللنجاح في موقف محدد نستخدم was able to أو managed to (وليس could): In the end, he was able to finish the race.\nmanaged to تعني «تمكّن بعد جهد/صعوبة».\ncouldn't تصلح للحالتين في النفي، وللمستقبل نستخدم will be able to لأن can ليس لها مستقبل."},"video":{"script":[{"time":"00:00–00:20","narration":"Hi, I’m Zaki! Today’s lesson is “He Managed to Finish! could, was able to, managed to”. Let’s learn past ability: could/couldn't (general ability), was/were able to and managed to (success on one occasion); will be able to (future) together.","on_screen":"GV-L05-U5-LS1 | He Managed to Finish! could, was able to, managed to","visual":"Zaki the Falcon flies in over the Leaf Green title card and lands next to the lesson title; soft chime."},{"time":"00:20–00:45","narration":"Listen to the start of “The Mountain Race”: When Yusuf was nine, he couldn't run more than one kilometer without stopping. But he could swim very well, and his coach, Mr. Look at the green words.","on_screen":"When Yusuf was nine, he couldn't run more than one kilometer without stopping. But he could swim very well, and his coach, Mr.","visual":"Image GV-L05-U5-LS1-IMG01 animates gently; target words glow Leaf Green as they are spoken."},{"time":"00:45–01:30","narration":"Use could / couldn't + base verb for general ability in the past – something you could do at any time: When I was six, I could swim. My grandpa could ride a horse. For success on one specific occasion, use was/were able to or managed to – NOT could: In the end, he was able to finish the race. She managed to score a goal in the last minute. (managed to shows it was difficult.)","on_screen":"general past ability → could / couldn't + base verb • one occasion, success → was/were able to / managed to + base verb • negative (general or one occasion) → couldn't","visual":"Grammar box builds line by line (form → meaning → use); Zaki points to each line."},{"time":"01:30–02:15","narration":"Here are some examples. My grandfather could ride a camel when he was five. I couldn't swim until I was ten. Despite the rain, we were able to finish the match.","on_screen":"My grandfather could ride a camel when he was five. / I couldn't swim until I was ten. / Despite the rain, we were able to finish the match.","visual":"Each example appears on a card with a small picture; the target part bounces once and turns green."},{"time":"02:15–02:45","narration":"Be careful! Some learners say: “Yesterday he could win the match.” That’s not right. We say: “Yesterday he was able to win / managed to win the match.” One specific success → was able to / managed to.","on_screen":"✗ Yesterday he could win the match.   ✓ Yesterday he was able to win / managed to win the match.","visual":"A red cross stamps the wrong sentence; it slides away and the correct sentence appears with a green tick."},{"time":"02:45–03:05","narration":"Quick check: \"He managed to finish.\" Was it easy? … Think! … No – it was difficult, but he succeeded. Great job! Now you can say: I can say \"I could swim when I was six.\"","on_screen":"Quick check ✔  |  I can: I can say \"I could swim when I was six.\"","visual":"Three-second pause with a countdown ring; Zaki gives a wing \"thumbs up\" and confetti in the programme colours."}],"captions_ar":["مرحبًا، أنا زكي! درس اليوم: past ability: could/couldn't (general ability), was/were able to and managed to (success on one occasion); will be able to (future).","استمع وانتبه إلى الكلمات الملوّنة بالأخضر.","could + المصدر للقدرة العامة في الماضي: When I was six, I could swim.","لاحظ الأمثلة: الجزء الملوّن هو القاعدة.","انتبه! الخطأ: Yesterday he could win the match. — الصحيح: Yesterday he was able to win / managed to win the match.","سؤال سريع… فكّر ثم أجب. أحسنت! الآن تستطيع: أستطيع أن أقول I could swim when I was six."]},"texts":[{"type":"reading","title":"The Mountain Race","body":"When Yusuf was nine, he couldn't run more than one kilometer without stopping. But he could swim very well, and his coach, Mr. Hamdan, noticed his stamina.\nTwo years ago, Yusuf decided to take on a new challenge: a mountain race near Taif.\nThe training was hard. At first, he couldn't keep up with the older runners, and he wanted to give up. But his coach said, \"If you train every day, you'll get stronger.\"\nOn the day of the race, it was very hot, and the road was steep. Halfway up the mountain, Yusuf hurt his ankle. He couldn't run fast anymore, but he managed to keep going.\nHundreds of spectators were cheering. In the end, he was able to finish the race in under two hours. He didn't win a trophy, but he managed to beat his own best time.\n\"I couldn't believe it!\" he says. \"Next year, I hope I'll be able to win.\"","word_count":157,"audio_id":"AUD-GV-L05-U5-LS1-T1"}],"activities":[{"id":"GV-L05-U5-LS1-A1","type":"mcq","instructions":"Controlled practice: Choose the correct answer.","items":[{"q":"When my brother was five, he ___ ride a bike without help.","options":["managed","could","was able","can"],"answer":"could","explanation":"General past ability → could + base verb.","explanation_ar":"القدرة العامة ← could."},{"q":"The match was difficult, but in the end we ___ win.","options":["manage","could","managed to","can"],"answer":"managed to","explanation":"Success after difficulty → managed to + base verb.","explanation_ar":"النجاح بعد صعوبة ← managed to."},{"q":"After the injury, the striker ___ run for two weeks.","options":["couldn't","can't to","didn't manage","wasn't able"],"answer":"couldn't","explanation":"Negative ability → couldn't.","explanation_ar":"النفي ← couldn't."},{"q":"Next year, I ___ swim across the lake.","options":["will be able to","can to","will can","could to"],"answer":"will be able to","explanation":"Future ability → will be able to.","explanation_ar":"المستقبل ← will be able to."},{"q":"Despite the strong wind, the pilot ___ land the plane safely.","options":["could","was able to","is able","can"],"answer":"was able to","explanation":"One specific success → was/were able to.","explanation_ar":"النجاح في موقف محدد ← was able to."},{"q":"My great-grandmother ___ read the Quran beautifully.","options":["was able","can to","managed to","could"],"answer":"could","explanation":"General past ability → could + base verb.","explanation_ar":"القدرة العامة ← could."}]},{"id":"GV-L05-U5-LS1-B1","type":"gap_fill","instructions":"Guided practice: Complete with could, couldn't, was/were able to or managed to. Word bank: couldn't, managed to, was able to","items":[{"q":"When I was little, I ______ (not / swim), but now I'm on the swimming team.","options":[],"answer":"couldn't","explanation":"Negative ability → couldn't.","explanation_ar":"النفي ← couldn't."},{"q":"The test was very hard, but Noura ______ (succeed in) answer every question.","options":[],"answer":"managed to","explanation":"Success after difficulty → managed to + base verb.","explanation_ar":"النجاح بعد صعوبة ← managed to."},{"q":"Thanks to the coach's advice, our team ______ (succeed) score in the last minute.","options":[],"answer":"was able to","explanation":"One specific success → was/were able to.","explanation_ar":"النجاح في موقف محدد ← was able to."}]},{"id":"GV-L05-U5-LS1-B2","type":"open","instructions":"Guided practice: Correct the mistake.","items":[{"q":"Yesterday, after a long game, our team could beat the champions.","options":[],"answer":"Yesterday, after a long game, our team managed to beat the champions.","explanation":"could → managed to. Success after difficulty → managed to + base verb.","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: could → managed to. النجاح بعد صعوبة ← managed to."},{"q":"In two years, I will can play for the school team.","options":[],"answer":"In two years, I will be able to play for the school team.","explanation":"will can → will be able to. Future ability → will be able to.","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: will can → will be able to. المستقبل ← will be able to."},{"q":"My uncle could to speak three languages when he was young.","options":[],"answer":"My uncle could speak three languages when he was young.","explanation":"could to → could. General past ability → could + base verb.","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: could to → could. القدرة العامة ← could."}]},{"id":"GV-L05-U5-LS1-C1","type":"open","instructions":"Freer practice: Write about you.","items":[{"q":"Something you could do when you were six: ___","options":[],"answer":"When I was six, I could ride a bike.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses past ability: could/couldn't (general ability), was/were able to and managed to (success on one occasion); will be able to (future) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Something you couldn't do last year, but you can do now: ___","options":[],"answer":"Last year I couldn't swim, but now I can.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses past ability: could/couldn't (general ability), was/were able to and managed to (success on one occasion); will be able to (future) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"A difficult thing you managed to do: ___","options":[],"answer":"I managed to finish a 5 km race last month.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses past ability: could/couldn't (general ability), was/were able to and managed to (success on one occasion); will be able to (future) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Something you were able to do in a recent competition: ___","options":[],"answer":"In the last math contest, I was able to solve every question.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses past ability: could/couldn't (general ability), was/were able to and managed to (success on one occasion); will be able to (future) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Something you hope you'll be able to do in five years: ___","options":[],"answer":"In five years, I hope I'll be able to play for a professional club.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses past ability: could/couldn't (general ability), was/were able to and managed to (success on one occasion); will be able to (future) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."}]},{"id":"GV-L05-U5-LS1-D1","type":"open","instructions":"Production: My challenge story.","items":[{"q":"Write 2 sentences about your general abilities as a young child (could / couldn't).","options":[],"answer":"When I was four, I could count to 100. I couldn't tie my shoelaces.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses past ability: could/couldn't (general ability), was/were able to and managed to (success on one occasion); will be able to (future) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write 3 sentences about a difficult day and how you succeeded (managed to / was able to).","options":[],"answer":"Last winter, my bike broke on the way to school. I couldn't ride it. But I managed to push it all the way and was able to arrive on time.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses past ability: could/couldn't (general ability), was/were able to and managed to (success on one occasion); will be able to (future) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write one sentence about the future with will be able to.","options":[],"answer":"After this course, I'll be able to speak in front of the class confidently.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses past ability: could/couldn't (general ability), was/were able to and managed to (success on one occasion); will be able to (future) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."}]}],"game":{"name":"Ball Toss Sentences","mechanic":"spin_wheel","rules":["Students stand in a circle. The teacher says a cue word and throws the ball.","The catcher makes a sentence with the cue word and past ability: could/couldn't (general ability), was/were able to and managed to (success on one occasion); will be able to (future).","If it is correct, the catcher chooses the next cue word and throws the ball to a new student.","If it is not correct, classmates help fix it, and the same student tries again."],"data":[{"label":"could"},{"label":"couldn't"},{"label":"was able to"},{"label":"managed to"},{"label":"will be able to"},{"label":"should"},{"label":"ought to"},{"label":"had better (not)"}]},"exit_quiz":[{"q":"My uncle ___ climb mountains easily when he was young.","options":["managed","was able","could","can to"],"answer":"could","explanation":"General past ability → could + base verb.","explanation_ar":"القدرة العامة ← could."},{"q":"The road was blocked, but we ___ find another way home.","options":["can","manage","could","managed to"],"answer":"managed to","explanation":"Success after difficulty → managed to + base verb.","explanation_ar":"النجاح بعد صعوبة ← managed to."},{"q":"","options":["The rescue team able to reach the climbers before dark.","The rescue team was able to reach the climbers before dark.","The rescue team was able reach the climbers before dark.","The rescue team could reach the climbers before dark yesterday."],"answer":"The rescue team was able to reach the climbers before dark.","explanation":"One specific success → was/were able to.","explanation_ar":"النجاح في موقف محدد ← was able to."},{"q":"She ___ sleep last night because of the noise.","options":["wasn't able","didn't manage","can't to","couldn't"],"answer":"couldn't","explanation":"Negative ability → couldn't.","explanation_ar":"النفي ← couldn't."},{"q":"","options":["When you finish the course, you'll be able to dive.","When you finish the course, you'll be able dive.","When you finish the course, you'll can dive.","When you finish the course, you be able to dive."],"answer":"When you finish the course, you'll be able to dive.","explanation":"Future ability → will be able to.","explanation_ar":"المستقبل ← will be able to."},{"q":"","options":["Hamad managed to score twice in the final.","Hamad managed scoring twice in the final.","Hamad was managed to score twice in the final.","Hamad managed score twice in the final."],"answer":"Hamad managed to score twice in the final.","explanation":"Success after difficulty → managed to + base verb.","explanation_ar":"النجاح بعد صعوبة ← managed to."},{"q":"Were you ___ to buy tickets for the match?","options":["managed","could","able","can"],"answer":"able","explanation":"One specific success → was/were able to.","explanation_ar":"النجاح في موقف محدد ← was able to."},{"q":"My little sister ___ talk when she was one year old.","options":["managed to","could","was able","can to"],"answer":"could","explanation":"General past ability → could + base verb.","explanation_ar":"القدرة العامة ← could."},{"q":"Which sentence shows success after difficulty?","options":["Despite the pain, Saad managed to finish the race.","Saad will be able to run next year.","Saad could run fast when he was ten.","Saad can run fast."],"answer":"Despite the pain, Saad managed to finish the race.","explanation":"Success after difficulty → managed to + base verb.","explanation_ar":"النجاح بعد صعوبة ← managed to."},{"q":"We trained hard, so we ___ win the cup this year.","options":["was able","can to","were able to","could"],"answer":"were able to","explanation":"One specific success → was/were able to.","explanation_ar":"النجاح في موقف محدد ← was able to."}],"images":[{"id":"GV-L05-U5-LS1-IMG01","prompt":"Yusuf running up a steep mountain road near Taif with a bandaged ankle, spectators cheering with flags, Coach Hamdan holding a stopwatch at the finish line. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as young teenagers aged 12–14, slightly more detailed, energetic composition. Landscape 16:9.","alt":"Yusuf running up a steep mountain road near Taif with a bandaged ankle, spectators cheering with flags, Coach Hamdan holding a stopwatch at the finish line"},{"id":"GV-L05-U5-LS1-IMG02","prompt":"clean background panel for a grammar box about past ability: could/couldn't (general ability), was/were able to and managed to (success on one occasion); will be able to (future), with Zaki the Falcon pointing at a blank board on the right; leave large empty space for text overlay. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as young teenagers aged 12–14, slightly more detailed, energetic composition. Square 1:1.","alt":"clean background panel for a grammar box about past ability: could/couldn't (general ability), was/were able to and managed to (success on one occasion)"},{"id":"GV-L05-U5-LS1-W01","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows athlete (a person who is good at sports and trains to compete); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as young teenagers aged 12–14, slightly more detailed, energetic composition. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing athlete: a person who is good at sports and trains to compete"},{"id":"GV-L05-U5-LS1-W02","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows coach (a person who trains a sports team or player); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as young teenagers aged 12–14, slightly more detailed, energetic composition. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing coach: a person who trains a sports team or player"},{"id":"GV-L05-U5-LS1-W03","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows opponent (the person or team you play against); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as young teenagers aged 12–14, slightly more detailed, energetic composition. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing opponent: the person or team you play against"},{"id":"GV-L05-U5-LS1-W04","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows tournament (a competition with many games or matches); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as young teenagers aged 12–14, slightly more detailed, energetic composition. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing tournament: a competition with many games or matches"},{"id":"GV-L05-U5-LS1-G01","prompt":"Card-back and board design for the classroom game “Ball Toss Sentences”: Zaki the Falcon icon in the centre, Leaf Green border with navy stars. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image. Portrait 2:3.","alt":"Game card design for Ball Toss Sentences"}],"audio":[{"id":"AUD-GV-L05-U5-LS1-W01","script":"athlete. athlete. The athlete trains six days a week.","voice":"en-US, friendly young-adult voice","speed":"0.95"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L05-U5-LS1-W02","script":"coach. coach. Our coach gives us great advice.","voice":"en-US, friendly young-adult voice","speed":"0.95"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L05-U5-LS1-W03","script":"opponent. opponent. Our opponents were very strong.","voice":"en-US, friendly young-adult voice","speed":"0.95"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L05-U5-LS1-W04","script":"tournament. tournament. Our school won the football tournament.","voice":"en-US, friendly young-adult voice","speed":"0.95"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L05-U5-LS1-T1","script":"When Yusuf was nine, he couldn't run more than one kilometer without stopping. But he could swim very well, and his coach, Mr. Hamdan, noticed his stamina. Two years ago, Yusuf decided to take on a new challenge: a mountain race near Taif. The training was hard. At first, he couldn't keep up with the older runners, and he wanted to give up. But his coach said, \"If you train every day, you'll get stronger.\" On the day of the race, it was very hot, and the road was steep. Halfway up the mountain, Yusuf hurt his ankle. He couldn't run fast anymore, but he managed to keep going. Hundreds of spectators were cheering. In the end, he was able to finish the race in under two hours. He didn't win a trophy, but he managed to beat his own best time. \"I couldn't believe it!\" he says. \"Next year, I hope I'll be able to win.\"","voice":"en-US, friendly young-adult voice","speed":"0.95"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L05-U5-LS1-VID","script":"Hi, I’m Zaki! Today’s lesson is “He Managed to Finish! could, was able to, managed to”. Let’s learn past ability: could/couldn't (general ability), was/were able to and managed to (success on one occasion); will be able to (future) together. Listen to the start of “The Mountain Race”: When Yusuf was nine, he couldn't run more than one kilometer without stopping. But he could swim very well, and his coach, Mr. Look at the green words. Use could / couldn't + base verb for general ability in the past – something you could do at any time: When I was six, I could swim. My grandpa could ride a horse. For success on one specific occasion, use was/were able to or managed to – NOT could: In the end, he was able to finish the race. She managed to score a goal in the last minute. (managed to shows it was difficult.) Here are some examples. My grandfather could ride a camel when he was five. I couldn't swim until I was ten. Despite the rain, we were able to finish the match. Be careful! Some learners say: “Yesterday he could win the match.” That’s not right. We say: “Yesterday he was able to win / managed to win the match.” One specific success → was able to / managed to. Quick check: \"He managed to finish.\" Was it easy? … Think! … No – it was difficult, but he succeeded. Great job! Now you can say: I can say \"I could swim when I was six.\"","voice":"en-US, Zaki the Falcon narrator voice (bright, friendly)","speed":"0.95"}]},{"id":"GV-L05-U5-LS2","title":"You'd Better Warm Up! should, ought to, had better","objectives":["Students will be able to give advice with should, shouldn't and ought to.","Students will be able to give strong advice or warnings with had better and had better not.","I can give advice: \"You should drink more water.\"","I can warn: \"You'd better not play with that injury.\""],"objectives_ar":["سيتمكن الطلاب من تقديم النصيحة بـ should وshouldn't وought to.","سيتمكن الطلاب من تقديم نصيحة قوية أو تحذير بـ had better وhad better not.","أستطيع أن أقدّم نصيحة: You should drink more water.","أستطيع أن أحذّر: You'd better not play with that injury."],"vocabulary":[{"word":"stamina","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈstæmənə/","definition":"the energy and strength to keep going for a long time","arabic":"قدرة على التحمّل","example":"Swimming builds your stamina.","image_id":"GV-L05-U5-LS2-W01","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L05-U5-LS2-W01"},{"word":"determination","pos":"noun","ipa":"/dɪˌtɝməˈneɪʃən/","definition":"not giving up when something is difficult","arabic":"عزيمة / إصرار","example":"With determination, you can reach your goal.","image_id":"GV-L05-U5-LS2-W02","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L05-U5-LS2-W02"},{"word":"injury","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈɪndʒɚi/","definition":"damage to the body, for example in an accident or sport","arabic":"إصابة","example":"He couldn't play because of a knee injury.","image_id":"GV-L05-U5-LS2-W03","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L05-U5-LS2-W03"},{"word":"defeat","pos":"noun","ipa":"/dɪˈfit/","definition":"losing a game or a competition","arabic":"هزيمة / خسارة","example":"The team learned a lot from the defeat.","image_id":"GV-L05-U5-LS2-W04","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L05-U5-LS2-W04"}],"explanation":{"text":"Use should / shouldn't + base verb to give advice or say what is right: You should warm up before you play. You shouldn't skip breakfast.\nought to + base verb means the same as should but is a bit more formal: You ought to drink more water. (Negative ought not to is rare; use shouldn't.)\nhad better ('d better) + base verb is stronger: a warning – something bad will happen if you don't: You'd better see a doctor (or your injury will get worse). Negative: had better not: You'd better not play today. It's about now / the future, not the past – even though it uses had.\nshould / shouldn't + base verb (advice)\nought to + base verb (advice, a bit formal)\nhad better / 'd better (not) + base verb (strong warning)\nNot: should to / had better to / had not better","examples":["Athletes should sleep at least eight hours.","You shouldn't play in the midday heat.","We ought to thank the referee.","You'd better wear a helmet, or you could hurt your head.","We'd better not be late for the bus.","Should I take up tennis or swimming?","You ought to stretch after every workout.","The team'd better train harder before the final."],"common_errors":["✗ You should to drink more water. → ✓ You should drink more water. (should + base verb (no to).)","✗ You had better to rest. → ✓ You had better rest. (had better + base verb.)","✗ You had not better play. → ✓ You had better not play. (Negative: had better not.)","✗ You ought rest. → ✓ You ought to rest. (ought to + base verb.)"],"arabic":"should وshouldn't + المصدر للنصيحة: You should warm up before you play.\nought to بمعنى should لكنها أكثر رسمية: You ought to drink more water.\nhad better ('d better) نصيحة قوية أو تحذير، والنفي had better not: You'd better not play today.\nلا نضع to بعد should أو had better، لكن نضعها بعد ought."},"video":{"script":[{"time":"00:00–00:20","narration":"Hi, I’m Zaki! Today’s lesson is “You'd Better Warm Up! should, ought to, had better”. Let’s learn advice and warnings: should/shouldn't, ought to, had better ('d better) / had better not together.","on_screen":"GV-L05-U5-LS2 | You'd Better Warm Up! should, ought to, had better","visual":"Zaki the Falcon flies in over the Leaf Green title card and lands next to the lesson title; soft chime."},{"time":"00:20–00:45","narration":"Remember these patterns: You should rest. • You ought to stretch. • You'd better hurry. You shouldn't eat junk food. • You'd better not be late. Should I join the swimming team? – Yes, you should.","on_screen":"You should rest. • You ought to stretch. • You'd better hurry. | You shouldn't eat junk food. • You'd better not be late. | Should I join the swimming team? – Yes, you should.","visual":"Pattern strips slide in from the left; the key words are highlighted in Leaf Green."},{"time":"00:45–01:30","narration":"Use should / shouldn't + base verb to give advice or say what is right: You should warm up before you play. You shouldn't skip breakfast. ought to + base verb means the same as should but is a bit more formal: You ought to drink more water. (Negative ought not to is rare; use shouldn't.)","on_screen":"should / shouldn't + base verb (advice) • ought to + base verb (advice, a bit formal) • had better / 'd better (not) + base verb (strong warning)","visual":"Grammar box builds line by line (form → meaning → use); Zaki points to each line."},{"time":"01:30–02:15","narration":"Here are some examples. Athletes should sleep at least eight hours. You shouldn't play in the midday heat. We ought to thank the referee.","on_screen":"Athletes should sleep at least eight hours. / You shouldn't play in the midday heat. / We ought to thank the referee.","visual":"Each example appears on a card with a small picture; the target part bounces once and turns green."},{"time":"02:15–02:45","narration":"Be careful! Some learners say: “You should to drink more water.” That’s not right. We say: “You should drink more water.” should + base verb (no to).","on_screen":"✗ You should to drink more water.   ✓ You should drink more water.","visual":"A red cross stamps the wrong sentence; it slides away and the correct sentence appears with a green tick."},{"time":"02:45–03:05","narration":"Quick check: \"You'd better see a doctor.\" Is this weak or strong advice? … Think! … Strong – a warning. Great job! Now you can say: I can give advice: \"You should drink more water.\"","on_screen":"Quick check ✔  |  I can: I can give advice: \"You should drink more water.\"","visual":"Three-second pause with a countdown ring; Zaki gives a wing \"thumbs up\" and confetti in the programme colours."}],"captions_ar":["مرحبًا، أنا زكي! درس اليوم: advice and warnings: should/shouldn't, ought to, had better ('d better) / had better not.","استمع وانتبه إلى الكلمات الملوّنة بالأخضر.","should وshouldn't + المصدر للنصيحة: You should warm up before you play.","لاحظ الأمثلة: الجزء الملوّن هو القاعدة.","انتبه! الخطأ: You should to drink more water. — الصحيح: You should drink more water.","سؤال سريع… فكّر ثم أجب. أحسنت! الآن تستطيع: أستطيع أن أقدّم نصيحة: You should drink more water."]},"texts":[],"activities":[{"id":"GV-L05-U5-LS2-A1","type":"mcq","instructions":"Controlled practice: Choose the correct answer.","items":[{"q":"You look tired. You ___ go to bed early tonight.","options":["had better to","ought","should","should to"],"answer":"should","explanation":"Advice → should / shouldn't + base verb.","explanation_ar":"النصيحة ← should."},{"q":"Athletes ___ to eat healthy food.","options":["must to","ought","had better","should"],"answer":"ought","explanation":"ought to + base verb (same as should).","explanation_ar":"ought to."},{"q":"It's 7:55! We ___ hurry, or we'll miss the start of the match.","options":["ought","'d better","'d better to","should to"],"answer":"'d better","explanation":"Strong advice / warning → had better + base verb.","explanation_ar":"التحذير ← had better."},{"q":"Your ankle is still swollen. You ___ play today.","options":["better not to","'d not better","had not better","'d better not"],"answer":"'d better not","explanation":"Negative warning → had better not.","explanation_ar":"had better not."},{"q":"___ I tell the coach about my injury? – Yes, you should.","options":["Should","Ought","Better","Had"],"answer":"Should","explanation":"Asking for advice → Should I…?","explanation_ar":"طلب النصيحة."},{"q":"You ___ run in the midday sun. It's dangerous.","options":["shouldn't","should to","had better to","ought not"],"answer":"shouldn't","explanation":"Advice → should / shouldn't + base verb.","explanation_ar":"النصيحة ← should."}]},{"id":"GV-L05-U5-LS2-B1","type":"gap_fill","instructions":"Guided practice: Complete with should, ought to, had better or had better not. Word bank: ought to, had better, had better not","items":[{"q":"Your knee hurts. You ______ (strong advice) see a doctor today.","options":[],"answer":"had better","explanation":"Strong advice / warning → had better + base verb.","explanation_ar":"التحذير ← had better."},{"q":"Players ______ (advice, formal) shake hands after the game.","options":[],"answer":"ought to","explanation":"ought to + base verb (same as should).","explanation_ar":"ought to."},{"q":"It's very windy. You ______ (warning) fly your drone.","options":[],"answer":"had better not","explanation":"Negative warning → had better not.","explanation_ar":"had better not."}]},{"id":"GV-L05-U5-LS2-B2","type":"open","instructions":"Guided practice: Give advice. Use the word in brackets.","items":[{"q":"My legs hurt after running. (should / stretch)  (advice)","options":[],"answer":"You should stretch after running.","explanation":"Advice → should / shouldn't + base verb.","explanation_ar":"النصيحة ← should."},{"q":"I always forget my water bottle. (ought to / put it in your bag)  (advice)","options":[],"answer":"You ought to put it in your bag the night before.","explanation":"ought to + base verb (same as should).","explanation_ar":"ought to."},{"q":"The pool is closing in five minutes. ('d better / get out)  (warning)","options":[],"answer":"You'd better get out now.","explanation":"Strong advice / warning → had better + base verb.","explanation_ar":"التحذير ← had better."}]},{"id":"GV-L05-U5-LS2-C1","type":"open","instructions":"Freer practice: Give advice to these athletes.","items":[{"q":"A runner who is always tired: ___","options":[],"answer":"You should sleep more and eat a good breakfast.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses advice and warnings: should/shouldn't, ought to, had better ('d better) / had better not correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"A goalkeeper who is afraid of the ball: ___","options":[],"answer":"You ought to practice with soft balls first.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses advice and warnings: should/shouldn't, ought to, had better ('d better) / had better not correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"A swimmer with an ear infection: ___","options":[],"answer":"You'd better not swim until it's better.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses advice and warnings: should/shouldn't, ought to, had better ('d better) / had better not correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"A player who gets angry with the referee: ___","options":[],"answer":"You shouldn't shout at the referee.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses advice and warnings: should/shouldn't, ought to, had better ('d better) / had better not correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"A friend who wants to join the basketball team: ___","options":[],"answer":"You should talk to the coach and go to the trials.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses advice and warnings: should/shouldn't, ought to, had better ('d better) / had better not correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."}]},{"id":"GV-L05-U5-LS2-D1","type":"open","instructions":"Production: Coach's tips card.","items":[{"q":"Write 3 tips for a new player (should / ought to).","options":[],"answer":"You should arrive ten minutes early. You ought to bring water. You should listen to the coach.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses advice and warnings: should/shouldn't, ought to, had better ('d better) / had better not correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write 2 warnings with had better / had better not.","options":[],"answer":"You'd better wear shin guards. You'd better not play with a fever.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses advice and warnings: should/shouldn't, ought to, had better ('d better) / had better not correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write a question asking for advice and its answer.","options":[],"answer":"Should I train every day? – No, you should rest at least one day a week.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses advice and warnings: should/shouldn't, ought to, had better ('d better) / had better not correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."}]}],"game":{"name":"Dice Sentences","mechanic":"spin_wheel","rules":["Turn over a cue card and roll the die.","Make a sentence with the cue in the form shown by the die.","The player on the left checks; a correct sentence wins the card.","The player with the most cards after 7 minutes wins."],"data":[{"cue":"when I was six / I / could / swim"},{"cue":"at first / he / couldn't / keep up"},{"cue":"in the end / she / manage / finish the race"},{"cue":"yesterday / we / be able to / win the match"},{"cue":"next year / I / be able to / run 10 km"},{"cue":"you / should / warm up first"},{"cue":"you / ought to / drink more water"},{"cue":"you / 'd better / not / play / with an injury"},{"cue":"should / I / join the team?"}]},"exit_quiz":[{"q":"You ___ wear sunscreen at the beach.","options":["should","ought","had better to","should to"],"answer":"should","explanation":"Advice → should / shouldn't + base verb.","explanation_ar":"النصيحة ← should."},{"q":"","options":["You ought to drank water during the match.","You ought to drinking water during the match.","You ought drink water during the match.","You ought to drink water during the match."],"answer":"You ought to drink water during the match.","explanation":"ought to + base verb (same as should).","explanation_ar":"ought to."},{"q":"The roads are icy. We ___ drive slowly.","options":["'d better","'d better to","should to","ought"],"answer":"'d better","explanation":"Strong advice / warning → had better + base verb.","explanation_ar":"التحذير ← had better."},{"q":"","options":["You hadn't better touch the hot pan.","You'd not better touch the hot pan.","You'd better not touch the hot pan.","You'd better not to touch the hot pan."],"answer":"You'd better not touch the hot pan.","explanation":"Negative warning → had better not.","explanation_ar":"had better not."},{"q":"","options":["Should we tell the referee?","Ought we tell the referee?","Do we should tell the referee?","Should we to tell the referee?"],"answer":"Should we tell the referee?","explanation":"Asking for advice → Should I…?","explanation_ar":"طلب النصيحة."},{"q":"Which sentence is a strong warning?","options":["You should visit Abha one day.","Maybe you ought to read more.","You should try volleyball.","You'd better stop now, or you'll hurt your back."],"answer":"You'd better stop now, or you'll hurt your back.","explanation":"Strong advice / warning → had better + base verb.","explanation_ar":"التحذير ← had better."},{"q":"Players ___ argue with the referee. It's bad sportsmanship.","options":["ought","should to","shouldn't","'d better"],"answer":"shouldn't","explanation":"Advice → should / shouldn't + base verb.","explanation_ar":"النصيحة ← should."},{"q":"\"You'd better hurry.\" means ___.","options":["It's better that you hurried yesterday.","You don't need to hurry.","Hurry now, or there will be a problem.","You hurried in the past."],"answer":"Hurry now, or there will be a problem.","explanation":"Strong advice / warning → had better + base verb.","explanation_ar":"التحذير ← had better."},{"q":"Saad ___ to apologize to his teammate.","options":["must to","ought","should","had better"],"answer":"ought","explanation":"ought to + base verb (same as should).","explanation_ar":"ought to."},{"q":"You have an exam tomorrow. You ___ stay up late.","options":["'d not better","'d better not","better not to","hadn't better to"],"answer":"'d better not","explanation":"Negative warning → had better not.","explanation_ar":"had better not."}],"images":[{"id":"GV-L05-U5-LS2-IMG01","prompt":"Coach Hamdan on a football field with a whistle and clipboard, giving advice to players who are stretching; a water bottle, an ice pack and a sign \"Warm up before you play!\". Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as young teenagers aged 12–14, slightly more detailed, energetic composition. Landscape 16:9.","alt":"Coach Hamdan on a football field with a whistle and clipboard, giving advice to players who are stretching; a water bottle, an ice pack and a sign \"Warm up before you play!\""},{"id":"GV-L05-U5-LS2-IMG02","prompt":"two-panel visual contrasting correct and incorrect use of advice and warnings: should/shouldn't, ought to, had better ('d better) / had better not: a green tick panel and a soft red cross panel with simple objects from the theme “Sport and Challenge”; empty space for text overlay. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as young teenagers aged 12–14, slightly more detailed, energetic composition. Square 1:1.","alt":"two-panel visual contrasting correct and incorrect use of advice and warnings: should/shouldn't, ought to, had better ('d better) / had better not: a green tick panel and a soft red cross panel with simple objects from the theme “Sport and Challenge”"},{"id":"GV-L05-U5-LS2-W01","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows stamina (the energy and strength to keep going for a long time); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as young teenagers aged 12–14, slightly more detailed, energetic composition. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing stamina: the energy and strength to keep going for a long time"},{"id":"GV-L05-U5-LS2-W02","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows determination (not giving up when something is difficult); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as young teenagers aged 12–14, slightly more detailed, energetic composition. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing determination: not giving up when something is difficult"},{"id":"GV-L05-U5-LS2-W03","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows injury (damage to the body, for example in an accident or sport); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as young teenagers aged 12–14, slightly more detailed, energetic composition. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing injury: damage to the body, for example in an accident or sport"},{"id":"GV-L05-U5-LS2-W04","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows defeat (losing a game or a competition); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as young teenagers aged 12–14, slightly more detailed, energetic composition. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing defeat: losing a game or a competition"},{"id":"GV-L05-U5-LS2-G01","prompt":"Card-back and board design for the classroom game “Dice Sentences”: Zaki the Falcon icon in the centre, Leaf Green border with navy stars. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image. Portrait 2:3.","alt":"Game card design for Dice Sentences"}],"audio":[{"id":"AUD-GV-L05-U5-LS2-W01","script":"stamina. stamina. Swimming builds your stamina.","voice":"en-US, friendly young-adult voice","speed":"0.95"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L05-U5-LS2-W02","script":"determination. determination. With determination, you can reach your goal.","voice":"en-US, friendly young-adult voice","speed":"0.95"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L05-U5-LS2-W03","script":"injury. injury. He couldn't play because of a knee injury.","voice":"en-US, friendly young-adult voice","speed":"0.95"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L05-U5-LS2-W04","script":"defeat. defeat. The team learned a lot from the defeat.","voice":"en-US, friendly young-adult voice","speed":"0.95"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L05-U5-LS2-VID","script":"Hi, I’m Zaki! Today’s lesson is “You'd Better Warm Up! should, ought to, had better”. Let’s learn advice and warnings: should/shouldn't, ought to, had better ('d better) / had better not together. Remember these patterns: You should rest. • You ought to stretch. • You'd better hurry. You shouldn't eat junk food. • You'd better not be late. Should I join the swimming team? – Yes, you should. Use should / shouldn't + base verb to give advice or say what is right: You should warm up before you play. You shouldn't skip breakfast. ought to + base verb means the same as should but is a bit more formal: You ought to drink more water. (Negative ought not to is rare; use shouldn't.) Here are some examples. Athletes should sleep at least eight hours. You shouldn't play in the midday heat. We ought to thank the referee. Be careful! Some learners say: “You should to drink more water.” That’s not right. We say: “You should drink more water.” should + base verb (no to). Quick check: \"You'd better see a doctor.\" Is this weak or strong advice? … Think! … Strong – a warning. Great job! Now you can say: I can give advice: \"You should drink more water.\"","voice":"en-US, Zaki the Falcon narrator voice (bright, friendly)","speed":"0.95"}]},{"id":"GV-L05-U5-LS3","title":"Warm Up, Work Out, Never Give Up! Sports Phrasal Verbs","objectives":["Students will be able to understand and use common sports phrasal verbs in context.","Students will be able to put an object pronoun in the middle of a separable phrasal verb (give it up, cheer him on).","I can say \"I warm up before I work out.\"","I can say \"Don't give it up!\""],"objectives_ar":["سيتمكن الطلاب من فهم الأفعال المركبة الرياضية الشائعة واستخدامها في السياق.","سيتمكن الطلاب من وضع الضمير المفعول في وسط الفعل المركب القابل للفصل (give it up, cheer him on).","أستطيع أن أقول I warm up before I work out.","أستطيع أن أقول Don't give it up!"],"vocabulary":[{"word":"victory","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈvɪktɚi/","definition":"winning a game or a competition","arabic":"فوز / انتصار","example":"The fans celebrated the victory.","image_id":"GV-L05-U5-LS3-W01","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L05-U5-LS3-W01"},{"word":"fitness","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈfɪtnəs/","definition":"being healthy and strong","arabic":"لياقة بدنية","example":"Fitness is important for every athlete.","image_id":"GV-L05-U5-LS3-W02","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L05-U5-LS3-W02"},{"word":"teammate","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈtiˌmeɪt/","definition":"a person on the same team as you","arabic":"زميل في الفريق","example":"My teammates passed me the ball.","image_id":"GV-L05-U5-LS3-W03","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L05-U5-LS3-W03"},{"word":"spectator","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈspɛkteɪtɚ/","definition":"a person who watches a sports event","arabic":"متفرّج","example":"Thousands of spectators filled the stadium.","image_id":"GV-L05-U5-LS3-W04","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L05-U5-LS3-W04"},{"word":"trophy","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈtroʊfi/","definition":"a cup or prize given to the winner","arabic":"كأس / جائزة","example":"The captain lifted the trophy.","image_id":"GV-L05-U5-LS3-W05","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L05-U5-LS3-W05"},{"word":"referee","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˌrɛfɚˈi/","definition":"the person who makes sure players follow the rules","arabic":"حَكَم","example":"The referee blew the whistle.","image_id":"GV-L05-U5-LS3-W06","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L05-U5-LS3-W06"}],"explanation":{"text":"A phrasal verb = verb + particle (up, out, on, in, off). The meaning is often different from the verb alone: give up = stop trying; work out = exercise.\nSports phrasal verbs: warm up (get ready with light exercise), cool down (slow exercise after sport), work out (exercise to get fit), give up (stop trying / stop doing), keep up with (go at the same speed), take up (start a new hobby or sport), join in (take part with others), cheer on (shout to support), sign up for (put your name down for), pull out of (leave a competition), carry on (continue), knock out (beat and remove from a competition).\nSome phrasal verbs are separable: with an object pronoun, the pronoun goes in the middle: I gave it up. (NOT (not: I gave up it)). We cheered them on. Three-part verbs (keep up with, sign up for, pull out of) don't separate: I signed up for it.\nCollocations/phrases: warm up ↔ cool down • work out • carry on | take up a sport • give up a habit • sign up for a club • pull out of a race | keep up with the others • join in the game • cheer the team on • knock a team out","examples":["Always warm up before a match.","My dad works out at the gym three times a week.","Don't give up! You can do it!","I couldn't keep up with the faster runners.","Sara took up archery last year.","The whole school came to cheer us on.","Rakan pulled out of the tournament because of an injury.","Our team was knocked out in the semi-final."],"common_errors":["✗ I want to give up it. → ✓ I want to give it up. (Pronoun in the middle.)","✗ I took up to swim last year. → ✓ I took up swimming last year. (take up + noun / -ing.)","✗ He can't keep up the other runners. → ✓ He can't keep up with the other runners. (keep up with + person.)","✗ Let's register in the club. → ✓ Let's sign up for the club. (sign up for.)"],"arabic":"الفعل المركب = فعل + حرف (up, out, on, in)، ومعناه يختلف غالبًا عن الفعل وحده: give up = يستسلم، work out = يتمرّن.\nwarm up (يحمّي)، cool down (يهدّئ الجسم بعد الرياضة)، take up (يبدأ هواية)، keep up with (يواكب)، sign up for (يسجّل في)، pull out of (ينسحب من)، cheer on (يشجّع).\nإذا كان المفعول ضميرًا في الفعل القابل للفصل نضعه في الوسط: give it up وليس give up it."},"video":{"script":[{"time":"00:00–00:20","narration":"Hi, I’m Zaki! Today’s lesson is “Warm Up, Work Out, Never Give Up! Sports Phrasal Verbs”. Let’s learn sports phrasal verbs (warm up, work out, give up, keep up with, take up, join in, cheer on, sign up for, pull out of, carry on, cool down, knock out); object pronoun position with separable phrasal verbs together.","on_screen":"GV-L05-U5-LS3 | Warm Up, Work Out, Never Give Up! Sports Phrasal Verbs","visual":"Zaki the Falcon flies in over the Leaf Green title card and lands next to the lesson title; soft chime."},{"time":"00:20–00:45","narration":"Meet some new words. victory: winning a game or a competition. fitness: being healthy and strong. teammate: a person on the same team as you.","on_screen":"victory | fitness | teammate","visual":"Each word card flips over to show its picture (vocabulary image briefs) and Arabic meaning."},{"time":"00:45–01:30","narration":"A phrasal verb = verb + particle (up, out, on, in, off). The meaning is often different from the verb alone: give up = stop trying; work out = exercise. Sports phrasal verbs: warm up (get ready with light exercise), cool down (slow exercise after sport), work out (exercise to get fit), give up (stop trying / stop doing), keep up with (go at the same speed), take up (start a new hobby or sport), join in (take part with others), cheer on (shout to support), sign up for (put your name down for), pull out of (leave a competition), carry on (continue), knock out (beat and remove from a competition).","on_screen":"sports phrasal verbs (warm up, work out, give up, keep up with, take up, join in, cheer on, sign up for, pull out of, carry on, cool down, knock out); object pronoun position with separable phrasal verbs","visual":"Grammar box builds line by line (form → meaning → use); Zaki points to each line."},{"time":"01:30–02:15","narration":"Here are some examples. Always warm up before a match. My dad works out at the gym three times a week. Don't give up! You can do it!","on_screen":"Always warm up before a match. / My dad works out at the gym three times a week. / Don't give up! You can do it!","visual":"Each example appears on a card with a small picture; the target part bounces once and turns green."},{"time":"02:15–02:45","narration":"Be careful! Some learners say: “I want to give up it.” That’s not right. We say: “I want to give it up.” Pronoun in the middle.","on_screen":"✗ I want to give up it.   ✓ I want to give it up.","visual":"A red cross stamps the wrong sentence; it slides away and the correct sentence appears with a green tick."},{"time":"02:45–03:05","narration":"Quick check: \"She took up archery.\" Did she start or stop archery? … Think! … She *started* it. Great job! Now you can say: I can say \"I warm up before I work out.\"","on_screen":"Quick check ✔  |  I can: I can say \"I warm up before I work out.\"","visual":"Three-second pause with a countdown ring; Zaki gives a wing \"thumbs up\" and confetti in the programme colours."}],"captions_ar":["مرحبًا، أنا زكي! درس اليوم: sports phrasal verbs (warm up, work out, give up, keep up with, take up, join in, cheer on, sign up for, pull out of, carry on, cool down, knock out); object pronoun position with separable phrasal verbs.","استمع وانتبه إلى الكلمات الملوّنة بالأخضر.","الفعل المركب = فعل + حرف (up, out, on, in)، ومعناه يختلف غالبًا عن الفعل وحده: give up = يستسلم، work out = يتمرّن.","لاحظ الأمثلة: الجزء الملوّن هو القاعدة.","انتبه! الخطأ: I want to give up it. — الصحيح: I want to give it up.","سؤال سريع… فكّر ثم أجب. أحسنت! الآن تستطيع: أستطيع أن أقول I warm up before I work out."]},"texts":[],"activities":[{"id":"GV-L05-U5-LS3-A1","type":"mcq","instructions":"Controlled practice: Choose the correct answer.","items":[{"q":"Before the match, the players ___ for fifteen minutes.","options":["work up","take up","give up","warm up"],"answer":"warm up","explanation":"Choose the phrasal verb with the right meaning.","explanation_ar":"المعنى الصحيح."},{"q":"My grandfather ___ golf when he retired.","options":["knocked out","gave up","took up","warmed up"],"answer":"took up","explanation":"Choose the phrasal verb with the right meaning.","explanation_ar":"المعنى الصحيح."},{"q":"I couldn't ___ the other cyclists on the hill.","options":["keep on with","keep out of","keep in with","keep up with"],"answer":"keep up with","explanation":"Three-part verbs keep together (keep up with, sign up for, pull out of).","explanation_ar":"الأفعال الثلاثية لا تنفصل."},{"q":"Don't stop now! ___ – you're almost at the finish line!","options":["Carry in","Carry on","Carry out","Carry off"],"answer":"Carry on","explanation":"Choose the right particle (up, out, on, in, off).","explanation_ar":"الحرف الصحيح."},{"q":"Football is too hard for me, but I won't ___.","options":["give it up","give up it","give it out","give out it"],"answer":"give it up","explanation":"Object pronoun goes in the middle (give it up).","explanation_ar":"الضمير في الوسط."},{"q":"Our school team was ___ in the first round of the tournament.","options":["knocked out","knocked on","knocked up","knocked in"],"answer":"knocked out","explanation":"Choose the right particle (up, out, on, in, off).","explanation_ar":"الحرف الصحيح."}]},{"id":"GV-L05-U5-LS3-B1","type":"match","instructions":"Guided practice: Match the phrasal verb to its meaning.","items":[{"q":"work out","options":["do slow exercise after sport","put your name down for a club or event","take part with other people","exercise to get fit","shout to support a player or team","leave a competition before it ends"],"answer":"exercise to get fit","explanation":"work out → exercise to get fit. Choose the phrasal verb with the right meaning.","explanation_ar":"المعنى الصحيح."},{"q":"join in","options":["do slow exercise after sport","put your name down for a club or event","take part with other people","exercise to get fit","shout to support a player or team","leave a competition before it ends"],"answer":"take part with other people","explanation":"join in → take part with other people. Choose the phrasal verb with the right meaning.","explanation_ar":"المعنى الصحيح."},{"q":"sign up for","options":["do slow exercise after sport","put your name down for a club or event","take part with other people","exercise to get fit","shout to support a player or team","leave a competition before it ends"],"answer":"put your name down for a club or event","explanation":"sign up for → put your name down for a club or event. Choose the phrasal verb with the right meaning.","explanation_ar":"المعنى الصحيح."},{"q":"pull out of","options":["do slow exercise after sport","put your name down for a club or event","take part with other people","exercise to get fit","shout to support a player or team","leave a competition before it ends"],"answer":"leave a competition before it ends","explanation":"pull out of → leave a competition before it ends. Choose the phrasal verb with the right meaning.","explanation_ar":"المعنى الصحيح."},{"q":"cheer on","options":["do slow exercise after sport","put your name down for a club or event","take part with other people","exercise to get fit","shout to support a player or team","leave a competition before it ends"],"answer":"shout to support a player or team","explanation":"cheer on → shout to support a player or team. Choose the phrasal verb with the right meaning.","explanation_ar":"المعنى الصحيح."},{"q":"cool down","options":["do slow exercise after sport","put your name down for a club or event","take part with other people","exercise to get fit","shout to support a player or team","leave a competition before it ends"],"answer":"do slow exercise after sport","explanation":"cool down → do slow exercise after sport. Choose the phrasal verb with the right meaning.","explanation_ar":"المعنى الصحيح."}]},{"id":"GV-L05-U5-LS3-C1","type":"open","instructions":"Freer practice: Write about your sports life.","items":[{"q":"A sport you want to take up: ___","options":[],"answer":"I want to take up rock climbing.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses sports phrasal verbs (warm up, work out, give up, keep up with, take up, join in, cheer on, sign up for, pull out of, carry on, cool down, knock out); object pronoun position with separable phrasal verbs correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"How you warm up before exercise: ___","options":[],"answer":"I warm up by jogging and stretching.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses sports phrasal verbs (warm up, work out, give up, keep up with, take up, join in, cheer on, sign up for, pull out of, carry on, cool down, knock out); object pronoun position with separable phrasal verbs correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Something you gave up because it was too hard: ___","options":[],"answer":"I gave up chess because it was too difficult for me.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses sports phrasal verbs (warm up, work out, give up, keep up with, take up, join in, cheer on, sign up for, pull out of, carry on, cool down, knock out); object pronoun position with separable phrasal verbs correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"A club you signed up for this year: ___","options":[],"answer":"I signed up for the school basketball club.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses sports phrasal verbs (warm up, work out, give up, keep up with, take up, join in, cheer on, sign up for, pull out of, carry on, cool down, knock out); object pronoun position with separable phrasal verbs correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Someone you cheer on: ___","options":[],"answer":"I cheer on my brother at his karate competitions.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses sports phrasal verbs (warm up, work out, give up, keep up with, take up, join in, cheer on, sign up for, pull out of, carry on, cool down, knock out); object pronoun position with separable phrasal verbs correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."}]},{"id":"GV-L05-U5-LS3-D1","type":"open","instructions":"Production: My sports diary.","items":[{"q":"Write 3 sentences about your weekly workout using 3 phrasal verbs.","options":[],"answer":"Every Saturday, I work out at the sports center. First, I warm up for ten minutes. After the workout, I cool down and stretch.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses sports phrasal verbs (warm up, work out, give up, keep up with, take up, join in, cheer on, sign up for, pull out of, carry on, cool down, knock out); object pronoun position with separable phrasal verbs correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write 2 sentences of encouragement for a tired teammate.","options":[],"answer":"Don't give up! Carry on – you can keep up with us!","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses sports phrasal verbs (warm up, work out, give up, keep up with, take up, join in, cheer on, sign up for, pull out of, carry on, cool down, knock out); object pronoun position with separable phrasal verbs correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write one sentence with a pronoun in the middle of a phrasal verb.","options":[],"answer":"The crowd cheered us on until the end.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses sports phrasal verbs (warm up, work out, give up, keep up with, take up, join in, cheer on, sign up for, pull out of, carry on, cool down, knock out); object pronoun position with separable phrasal verbs correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."}]}],"game":{"name":"Vocabulary Bingo","mechanic":"matching","rules":["Students write nine words from the list in their grid.","The teacher (or a student caller) reads a definition or Arabic meaning.","Students cross out the matching word.","Three in a row = \"Bingo!\" The winner must read the words and say what each means."],"data":[{"word":"athlete","match":"a person who is good at sports and trains to compete","arabic":"رياضي"},{"word":"coach","match":"a person who trains a sports team or player","arabic":"مدرّب"},{"word":"opponent","match":"the person or team you play against","arabic":"خصم / منافس"},{"word":"tournament","match":"a competition with many games or matches","arabic":"بطولة"},{"word":"stamina","match":"the energy and strength to keep going for a long time","arabic":"قدرة على التحمّل"},{"word":"determination","match":"not giving up when something is difficult","arabic":"عزيمة / إصرار"},{"word":"injury","match":"damage to the body, for example in an accident or sport","arabic":"إصابة"},{"word":"defeat","match":"losing a game or a competition","arabic":"هزيمة / خسارة"},{"word":"victory","match":"winning a game or a competition","arabic":"فوز / انتصار"},{"word":"fitness","match":"being healthy and strong","arabic":"لياقة بدنية"},{"word":"teammate","match":"a person on the same team as you","arabic":"زميل في الفريق"},{"word":"spectator","match":"a person who watches a sports event","arabic":"متفرّج"},{"word":"trophy","match":"a cup or prize given to the winner","arabic":"كأس / جائزة"},{"word":"referee","match":"the person who makes sure players follow the rules","arabic":"حَكَم"}]},"exit_quiz":[{"q":"The fans came to ___ their team ___.","options":["cheer / out","cheer / off","cheer / in","cheer / on"],"answer":"cheer / on","explanation":"Choose the phrasal verb with the right meaning.","explanation_ar":"المعنى الصحيح."},{"q":"I've ___ the swimming competition. My name is on the list!","options":["signed on with","signed out of","signed in for","signed up for"],"answer":"signed up for","explanation":"Three-part verbs keep together (keep up with, sign up for, pull out of).","explanation_ar":"الأفعال الثلاثية لا تنفصل."},{"q":"He hurt his back, so he had to ___ the race.","options":["pull out of","pull off for","pull in of","pull up with"],"answer":"pull out of","explanation":"Three-part verbs keep together (keep up with, sign up for, pull out of).","explanation_ar":"الأفعال الثلاثية لا تنفصل."},{"q":"My father ___ at the gym every morning.","options":["works in","works out","works up","works off"],"answer":"works out","explanation":"Choose the right particle (up, out, on, in, off).","explanation_ar":"الحرف الصحيح."},{"q":"","options":["Smoking is bad for you. You should give it out.","Smoking is bad for you. You should give it up.","Smoking is bad for you. You should up give it.","Smoking is bad for you. You should give up it."],"answer":"Smoking is bad for you. You should give it up.","explanation":"Object pronoun goes in the middle (give it up).","explanation_ar":"الضمير في الوسط."},{"q":"Everybody was playing, so I ___ too.","options":["joined in","joined up","joined on","joined out"],"answer":"joined in","explanation":"Choose the phrasal verb with the right meaning.","explanation_ar":"المعنى الصحيح."},{"q":"After a hard run, you should ___ slowly.","options":["take up","cool down","knock out","warm up"],"answer":"cool down","explanation":"Choose the phrasal verb with the right meaning.","explanation_ar":"المعنى الصحيح."},{"q":"","options":["The little kids couldn't keep us up with.","The little kids couldn't keep up us.","The little kids couldn't keep with up us.","The little kids couldn't keep up with us."],"answer":"The little kids couldn't keep up with us.","explanation":"Three-part verbs keep together (keep up with, sign up for, pull out of).","explanation_ar":"الأفعال الثلاثية لا تنفصل."},{"q":"The crowd ___ even when we were losing.","options":["cheered us out","cheered on us","cheered us on","cheered up on us"],"answer":"cheered us on","explanation":"Object pronoun goes in the middle (give it up).","explanation_ar":"الضمير في الوسط."},{"q":"\"Never give up\" means ___.","options":["Never warm up.","Never take part.","Never stop trying.","Never start a sport."],"answer":"Never stop trying.","explanation":"Choose the phrasal verb with the right meaning.","explanation_ar":"المعنى الصحيح."}],"images":[{"id":"GV-L05-U5-LS3-IMG01","prompt":"A sports center noticeboard with sign-up sheets, a group warming up, a girl cheering her sister on at the pool, and a karate class where one boy is about to give up while his friend encourages him. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as young teenagers aged 12–14, slightly more detailed, energetic composition. Landscape 16:9.","alt":"A sports center noticeboard with sign-up sheets, a group warming up, a girl cheering her sister on at the pool, and a karate class where one boy is about to give up while his friend encourages him"},{"id":"GV-L05-U5-LS3-IMG02","prompt":"word-web visual: a central circle with branches to 6 small picture icons of the lesson words; empty labels. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as young teenagers aged 12–14, slightly more detailed, energetic composition. Square 1:1.","alt":"word-web visual: a central circle with branches to 6 small picture icons of the lesson words"},{"id":"GV-L05-U5-LS3-W01","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows victory (winning a game or a competition); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as young teenagers aged 12–14, slightly more detailed, energetic composition. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing victory: winning a game or a competition"},{"id":"GV-L05-U5-LS3-W02","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows fitness (being healthy and strong); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as young teenagers aged 12–14, slightly more detailed, energetic composition. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing fitness: being healthy and strong"},{"id":"GV-L05-U5-LS3-W03","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows teammate (a person on the same team as you); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as young teenagers aged 12–14, slightly more detailed, energetic composition. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing teammate: a person on the same team as you"},{"id":"GV-L05-U5-LS3-W04","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows spectator (a person who watches a sports event); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as young teenagers aged 12–14, slightly more detailed, energetic composition. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing spectator: a person who watches a sports event"},{"id":"GV-L05-U5-LS3-W05","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows trophy (a cup or prize given to the winner); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as young teenagers aged 12–14, slightly more detailed, energetic composition. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing trophy: a cup or prize given to the winner"},{"id":"GV-L05-U5-LS3-W06","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows referee (the person who makes sure players follow the rules); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as young teenagers aged 12–14, slightly more detailed, energetic composition. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing referee: the person who makes sure players follow the rules"},{"id":"GV-L05-U5-LS3-G01","prompt":"Card-back and board design for the classroom game “Vocabulary Bingo”: Zaki the Falcon icon in the centre, Leaf Green border with navy stars. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image. Portrait 2:3.","alt":"Game card design for Vocabulary Bingo"}],"audio":[{"id":"AUD-GV-L05-U5-LS3-W01","script":"victory. victory. The fans celebrated the victory.","voice":"en-US, friendly young-adult voice","speed":"0.95"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L05-U5-LS3-W02","script":"fitness. fitness. Fitness is important for every athlete.","voice":"en-US, friendly young-adult voice","speed":"0.95"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L05-U5-LS3-W03","script":"teammate. teammate. My teammates passed me the ball.","voice":"en-US, friendly young-adult voice","speed":"0.95"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L05-U5-LS3-W04","script":"spectator. spectator. Thousands of spectators filled the stadium.","voice":"en-US, friendly young-adult voice","speed":"0.95"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L05-U5-LS3-W05","script":"trophy. trophy. The captain lifted the trophy.","voice":"en-US, friendly young-adult voice","speed":"0.95"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L05-U5-LS3-W06","script":"referee. referee. The referee blew the whistle.","voice":"en-US, friendly young-adult voice","speed":"0.95"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L05-U5-LS3-VID","script":"Hi, I’m Zaki! Today’s lesson is “Warm Up, Work Out, Never Give Up! Sports Phrasal Verbs”. Let’s learn sports phrasal verbs (warm up, work out, give up, keep up with, take up, join in, cheer on, sign up for, pull out of, carry on, cool down, knock out); object pronoun position with separable phrasal verbs together. Meet some new words. victory: winning a game or a competition. fitness: being healthy and strong. teammate: a person on the same team as you. A phrasal verb = verb + particle (up, out, on, in, off). The meaning is often different from the verb alone: give up = stop trying; work out = exercise. Sports phrasal verbs: warm up (get ready with light exercise), cool down (slow exercise after sport), work out (exercise to get fit), give up (stop trying / stop doing), keep up with (go at the same speed), take up (start a new hobby or sport), join in (take part with others), cheer on (shout to support), sign up for (put your name down for), pull out of (leave a competition), carry on (continue), knock out (beat and remove from a competition). Here are some examples. Always warm up before a match. My dad works out at the gym three times a week. Don't give up! You can do it! Be careful! Some learners say: “I want to give up it.” That’s not right. We say: “I want to give it up.” Pronoun in the middle. Quick check: \"She took up archery.\" Did she start or stop archery? … Think! … She *started* it. Great job! Now you can say: I can say \"I warm up before I work out.\"","voice":"en-US, Zaki the Falcon narrator voice (bright, friendly)","speed":"0.95"}]},{"id":"GV-L05-U5-LS4","title":"Omar and Saad Play, Every Player Trains: Agreement with Compound Subjects","objectives":["Students will be able to make the verb agree with subjects joined by and, or and nor.","Students will be able to use singular verbs with each/every and with collective nouns in American English.","I can write \"Omar and Saad play\" and \"Every player trains.\"","I can choose \"Neither the coach nor the players were…\""],"objectives_ar":["سيتمكن الطلاب من جعل الفعل يتوافق مع الفاعل المركب بـ and وor وnor.","سيتمكن الطلاب من استخدام فعل مفرد مع each/every ومع أسماء الجمع في الإنجليزية الأمريكية.","أستطيع أن أكتب Omar and Saad play وEvery player trains.","أستطيع أن أختار Neither the coach nor the players were…"],"vocabulary":[{"word":"athlete","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈæθˌlit/","definition":"a person who is good at sports and trains to compete","arabic":"رياضي","example":"The athlete trains six days a week.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"coach","pos":"noun","ipa":"/koʊtʃ/","definition":"a person who trains a sports team or player","arabic":"مدرّب","example":"Our coach gives us great advice.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"opponent","pos":"noun","ipa":"/əˈpoʊnənt/","definition":"the person or team you play against","arabic":"خصم / منافس","example":"Our opponents were very strong.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"tournament","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈtʊrnəmənt/","definition":"a competition with many games or matches","arabic":"بطولة","example":"Our school won the football tournament.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"stamina","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈstæmənə/","definition":"the energy and strength to keep going for a long time","arabic":"قدرة على التحمّل","example":"Swimming builds your stamina.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"determination","pos":"noun","ipa":"/dɪˌtɝməˈneɪʃən/","definition":"not giving up when something is difficult","arabic":"عزيمة / إصرار","example":"With determination, you can reach your goal.","image_id":"","audio_id":""}],"explanation":{"text":"Two subjects joined by and = plural verb: Omar and Saad play tennis. My coach and my teammate are proud of me.\nWith or, either…or, neither…nor, the verb agrees with the subject nearest to it: Either the coach or the players carry the bags. Neither the players nor the coach was happy.\nEach and every + singular noun = singular verb: Every player trains on Monday. Each team has eleven players. In American English, collective nouns like team, family, class, crowd usually take a singular verb: The team is ready. With along with / as well as, the verb agrees with the first subject: The captain, along with his teammates, is here.\nA and B → plural verb (are, play, have)\nA or/nor B → verb agrees with B (the nearer one)\neach / every + noun → singular verb\nteam / family / class → singular (American English)","examples":["Yusuf and his brother run every morning.","Either Lina or her sisters are coming to cheer us on.","Neither the players nor the coach was late.","Every athlete needs rest.","Each of the trophies is made of silver.","Our team is in the final!","The coach, along with the players, is on the bus.","Fitness and determination are important for success."],"common_errors":["✗ Omar and Saad plays football. → ✓ Omar and Saad play football. (A and B → plural.)","✗ Every player have a number. → ✓ Every player has a number. (every → singular.)","✗ Neither the coach nor the players was ready. → ✓ Neither the coach nor the players were ready. (Agrees with the nearer subject (players).)","✗ The team are winning. (American English) → ✓ The team is winning. (Collective noun → singular in American English.)"],"arabic":"فاعلان مرتبطان بـ and يأخذان فعل جمع: Omar and Saad play.\nمع or وeither…or وneither…nor يتوافق الفعل مع الفاعل الأقرب إليه.\neach وevery + اسم مفرد يأخذان فعلًا مفردًا: Every player trains.\nفي الإنجليزية الأمريكية نعامل team وfamily وclass معاملة المفرد: The team is ready."},"video":{"script":[{"time":"00:00–00:20","narration":"Hi, I’m Zaki! Today’s lesson is “Omar and Saad Play, Every Player Trains: Agreement with Compound Subjects”. Let’s learn subject–verb agreement with compound subjects (and; or / nor / either…or / neither…nor), each/every, collective nouns (team, family, class) and along with / as well as together.","on_screen":"GV-L05-U5-LS4 | Omar and Saad Play, Every Player Trains: Agreement with Compound Subjects","visual":"Zaki the Falcon flies in over the Leaf Green title card and lands next to the lesson title; soft chime."},{"time":"00:20–00:45","narration":"Two subjects joined by and = plural verb: Omar and Saad play tennis. My coach and my teammate are proud of me.","on_screen":"subject–verb agreement with compound subjects (and; or / nor / either…or / neither…nor), each/every, collective nouns (team, family, class) and along with / as well as","visual":"Pattern strips slide in from the left; the key words are highlighted in Leaf Green."},{"time":"00:45–01:30","narration":"Two subjects joined by and = plural verb: Omar and Saad play tennis. My coach and my teammate are proud of me. With or, either…or, neither…nor, the verb agrees with the subject nearest to it: Either the coach or the players carry the bags. Neither the players nor the coach was happy.","on_screen":"A and B → plural verb (are, play, have) • A or/nor B → verb agrees with B (the nearer one) • each / every + noun → singular verb","visual":"Grammar box builds line by line (form → meaning → use); Zaki points to each line."},{"time":"01:30–02:15","narration":"Here are some examples. Yusuf and his brother run every morning. Either Lina or her sisters are coming to cheer us on. Neither the players nor the coach was late.","on_screen":"Yusuf and his brother run every morning. / Either Lina or her sisters are coming to cheer us on. / Neither the players nor the coach was late.","visual":"Each example appears on a card with a small picture; the target part bounces once and turns green."},{"time":"02:15–02:45","narration":"Be careful! Some learners say: “Omar and Saad plays football.” That’s not right. We say: “Omar and Saad play football.” A and B → plural.","on_screen":"✗ Omar and Saad plays football.   ✓ Omar and Saad play football.","visual":"A red cross stamps the wrong sentence; it slides away and the correct sentence appears with a green tick."},{"time":"02:45–03:05","narration":"Quick check: \"Either Lina or her sisters ___ coming.\" Which subject is nearer to the verb? … Think! … *her sisters* → *are*. Great job! Now you can say: I can write \"Omar and Saad play\" and \"Every player trains.\"","on_screen":"Quick check ✔  |  I can: I can write \"Omar and Saad play\" and \"Every player trains.\"","visual":"Three-second pause with a countdown ring; Zaki gives a wing \"thumbs up\" and confetti in the programme colours."}],"captions_ar":["مرحبًا، أنا زكي! درس اليوم: subject–verb agreement with compound subjects (and; or / nor / either…or / neither…nor), each/every, collective nouns (team, family, class) and along with / as well as.","استمع وانتبه إلى الكلمات الملوّنة بالأخضر.","فاعلان مرتبطان بـ and يأخذان فعل جمع: Omar and Saad play.","لاحظ الأمثلة: الجزء الملوّن هو القاعدة.","انتبه! الخطأ: Omar and Saad plays football. — الصحيح: Omar and Saad play football.","سؤال سريع… فكّر ثم أجب. أحسنت! الآن تستطيع: أستطيع أن أكتب Omar and Saad play وEvery player trains."]},"texts":[{"type":"reading","title":"Sports Day Report (editing passage)","body":"Sports Day was a great success. Omar and Yusuf was the fastest runners. Every class have its own color. Either the teachers or the principal give the medals at the end. The crowd are very excited. Neither the referees nor the coach were tired after the long day. Fitness and fun is the goals of the day!","word_count":57,"audio_id":""}],"activities":[{"id":"GV-L05-U5-LS4-A1","type":"mcq","instructions":"Controlled practice: Choose the correct verb.","items":[{"q":"Omar and his cousin ___ karate every Monday.","options":["practice","practicing","is practicing","practices"],"answer":"practice","explanation":"A and B → plural verb.","explanation_ar":"and ← فعل جمع."},{"q":"Every runner ___ a number on his shirt.","options":["wear","are wearing","wears","have worn"],"answer":"wears","explanation":"each / every → singular verb.","explanation_ar":"each/every ← مفرد."},{"q":"Either Sara or her sisters ___ going to the tournament.","options":["is","has","are","was"],"answer":"are","explanation":"or / nor → verb agrees with the nearer subject.","explanation_ar":"or/nor ← الفاعل الأقرب."},{"q":"Neither the players nor the coach ___ happy with the result.","options":["were","was","are","have"],"answer":"was","explanation":"or / nor → verb agrees with the nearer subject.","explanation_ar":"or/nor ← الفاعل الأقرب."},{"q":"Our basketball team ___ in the final this Thursday.","options":["have played","play","are playing","plays"],"answer":"plays","explanation":"team / family / class → singular verb (American English).","explanation_ar":"أسماء الجمع ← مفرد."},{"q":"The captain, along with his teammates, ___ on the bus.","options":["have been","is","were","are"],"answer":"is","explanation":"along with / as well as → verb agrees with the first subject.","explanation_ar":"along with ← الفاعل الأول."}]},{"id":"GV-L05-U5-LS4-B1","type":"gap_fill","instructions":"Guided practice: Write the correct form of the verb. Word bank: are, has, like","items":[{"q":"Stamina and determination ______ (be) important for athletes.","options":[],"answer":"are","explanation":"A and B → plural verb.","explanation_ar":"and ← فعل جمع."},{"q":"Each of the spectators ______ (have) a ticket.","options":[],"answer":"has","explanation":"each / every → singular verb.","explanation_ar":"each/every ← مفرد."},{"q":"Neither my brother nor my cousins ______ (like) swimming.","options":[],"answer":"like","explanation":"or / nor → verb agrees with the nearer subject.","explanation_ar":"or/nor ← الفاعل الأقرب."}]},{"id":"GV-L05-U5-LS4-B2","type":"open","instructions":"Guided practice: Correct the mistake.","items":[{"q":"My father and my uncle watches every match together.","options":[],"answer":"My father and my uncle watch every match together.","explanation":"watches → watch. A and B → plural verb.","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: watches → watch. and ← فعل جمع."},{"q":"Every teammate need a water bottle.","options":[],"answer":"Every teammate needs a water bottle.","explanation":"need → needs. each / every → singular verb.","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: need → needs. each/every ← مفرد."},{"q":"The whole class are going to the stadium.","options":[],"answer":"The whole class is going to the stadium.","explanation":"are → is. team / family / class → singular verb (American English).","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: are → is. أسماء الجمع ← مفرد."}]},{"id":"GV-L05-U5-LS4-C1","type":"open","instructions":"Freer practice: Edit the sports report.","items":[{"q":"Omar and Yusuf was the fastest runners.","options":[],"answer":"Omar and Yusuf were the fastest runners.","explanation":"was → were. A and B → plural verb.","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: was → were. and ← فعل جمع."},{"q":"Every class have its own color.","options":[],"answer":"Every class has its own color.","explanation":"have → has. A and B → plural verb.","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: have → has. and ← فعل جمع."},{"q":"Either the teachers or the principal give the medals at the end.","options":[],"answer":"Either the teachers or the principal gives the medals at the end.","explanation":"give → gives. A and B → plural verb.","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: give → gives. and ← فعل جمع."},{"q":"The crowd are very excited.","options":[],"answer":"The crowd is very excited.","explanation":"are → is. A and B → plural verb.","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: are → is. and ← فعل جمع."},{"q":"Neither the referees nor the coach were tired after the long day.","options":[],"answer":"Neither the referees nor the coach was tired after the long day.","explanation":"were → was. A and B → plural verb.","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: were → was. and ← فعل جمع."},{"q":"Fitness and fun is the goals of the day!","options":[],"answer":"Fitness and fun are the goals of the day!","explanation":"is → are. A and B → plural verb.","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: is → are. and ← فعل جمع."}]},{"id":"GV-L05-U5-LS4-D1","type":"open","instructions":"Production: Write about your team.","items":[{"q":"Write 2 sentences with \"and\" subjects.","options":[],"answer":"My friend and I play tennis on Fridays. Speed and strength are important in football.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses subject–verb agreement with compound subjects (and; or / nor / either…or / neither…nor), each/every, collective nouns (team, family, class) and along with / as well as correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write 2 sentences with each/every.","options":[],"answer":"Every player brings his own ball. Each match lasts ninety minutes.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses subject–verb agreement with compound subjects (and; or / nor / either…or / neither…nor), each/every, collective nouns (team, family, class) and along with / as well as correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write one sentence with neither…nor.","options":[],"answer":"Neither the players nor the coach wants to lose.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses subject–verb agreement with compound subjects (and; or / nor / either…or / neither…nor), each/every, collective nouns (team, family, class) and along with / as well as correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."}]}],"game":{"name":"Letter Ladder","mechanic":"word_builder","rules":["The leader chooses a word from the list and draws one blank line per letter.","Teams take turns guessing letters. Correct letters are written on the lines.","Each wrong guess removes one rung of the ladder (start with 6 rungs).","The team that completes the word scores 2 points and uses it in a sentence."],"data":[{"word":"athlete","clue":"a person who is good at sports and trains to compete"},{"word":"opponent","clue":"the person or team you play against"},{"word":"tournament","clue":"a competition with many games or matches"},{"word":"stamina","clue":"the energy and strength to keep going for a long time"},{"word":"determination","clue":"not giving up when something is difficult"},{"word":"referee","clue":"the person who makes sure players follow the rules"},{"word":"coach","clue":"a person who trains a sports team or player"},{"word":"injury","clue":"damage to the body, for example in an accident or sport"},{"word":"defeat","clue":"losing a game or a competition"},{"word":"victory","clue":"winning a game or a competition"},{"word":"fitness","clue":"being healthy and strong"}]},"exit_quiz":[{"q":"Saad and Rakan ___ on the same team.","options":["was","has","is","are"],"answer":"are","explanation":"A and B → plural verb.","explanation_ar":"and ← فعل جمع."},{"q":"Each competitor ___ three chances.","options":["gets","get","are getting","have"],"answer":"gets","explanation":"each / every → singular verb.","explanation_ar":"each/every ← مفرد."},{"q":"Neither the referee nor the players ___ the rule.","options":["has known","know","knows","is knowing"],"answer":"know","explanation":"or / nor → verb agrees with the nearer subject.","explanation_ar":"or/nor ← الفاعل الأقرب."},{"q":"","options":["My family is going to the match tonight.","My family am going to the match tonight.","My family are going to the match tonight.","My family be going to the match tonight."],"answer":"My family is going to the match tonight.","explanation":"team / family / class → singular verb (American English).","explanation_ar":"أسماء الجمع ← مفرد."},{"q":"","options":["Every boy and girl in the school have a sports uniform.","Every boy and girl in the school having a sports uniform.","Every boy and girl in the school are having a sports uniform.","Every boy and girl in the school has a sports uniform."],"answer":"Every boy and girl in the school has a sports uniform.","explanation":"each / every → singular verb.","explanation_ar":"each/every ← مفرد."},{"q":"Either the twins or their father ___ the car to the stadium.","options":["have driven","drives","are driving","drive"],"answer":"drives","explanation":"or / nor → verb agrees with the nearer subject.","explanation_ar":"or/nor ← الفاعل الأقرب."},{"q":"The coach, as well as the players, ___ excited about the final.","options":["is","were","are","have been"],"answer":"is","explanation":"along with / as well as → verb agrees with the first subject.","explanation_ar":"along with ← الفاعل الأول."},{"q":"The crowd ___ cheering loudly.","options":["are","were being","is","have"],"answer":"is","explanation":"team / family / class → singular verb (American English).","explanation_ar":"أسماء الجمع ← مفرد."},{"q":"","options":["Hard work and determination leading to success.","Hard work and determination lead to success.","Hard work and determination leads to success.","Hard work and determination is lead to success."],"answer":"Hard work and determination lead to success.","explanation":"A and B → plural verb.","explanation_ar":"and ← فعل جمع."},{"q":"Either my sisters or my mother ___ coming to the race.","options":["were","are","is","have"],"answer":"is","explanation":"or / nor → verb agrees with the nearer subject.","explanation_ar":"or/nor ← الفاعل الأقرب."}],"images":[{"id":"GV-L05-U5-LS4-IMG01","prompt":"Two or three of the programme characters in a scene about “Sport and Challenge” that shows subject–verb agreement with compound subjects (and; or / nor / either…or / neither…nor), each/every, collective nouns (team, family, class) and along with / as well as in action. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as young teenagers aged 12–14, slightly more detailed, energetic composition. Landscape 16:9.","alt":"Two or three of the programme characters in a scene about “Sport and Challenge” that shows subject–verb agreement with compound subjects (and; or / nor / either…or / neither…nor), each/every, collective nouns (team, family, class) and along with / as well as in action"},{"id":"GV-L05-U5-LS4-IMG02","prompt":"friendly editing-desk scene: a magnifying glass over a page with highlighted punctuation marks, Zaki holding a green pen. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as young teenagers aged 12–14, slightly more detailed, energetic composition. Square 1:1.","alt":"friendly editing-desk scene: a magnifying glass over a page with highlighted punctuation marks, Zaki holding a green pen"},{"id":"GV-L05-U5-LS4-G01","prompt":"Card-back and board design for the classroom game “Letter Ladder”: Zaki the Falcon icon in the centre, Leaf Green border with navy stars. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image. Portrait 2:3.","alt":"Game card design for Letter Ladder"}],"audio":[{"id":"AUD-GV-L05-U5-LS4-VID","script":"Hi, I’m Zaki! Today’s lesson is “Omar and Saad Play, Every Player Trains: Agreement with Compound Subjects”. Let’s learn subject–verb agreement with compound subjects (and; or / nor / either…or / neither…nor), each/every, collective nouns (team, family, class) and along with / as well as together. Two subjects joined by and = plural verb: Omar and Saad play tennis. My coach and my teammate are proud of me. Two subjects joined by and = plural verb: Omar and Saad play tennis. My coach and my teammate are proud of me. With or, either…or, neither…nor, the verb agrees with the subject nearest to it: Either the coach or the players carry the bags. Neither the players nor the coach was happy. Here are some examples. Yusuf and his brother run every morning. Either Lina or her sisters are coming to cheer us on. Neither the players nor the coach was late. Be careful! Some learners say: “Omar and Saad plays football.” That’s not right. We say: “Omar and Saad play football.” A and B → plural. Quick check: \"Either Lina or her sisters ___ coming.\" Which subject is nearer to the verb? … Think! … *her sisters* → *are*. Great job! Now you can say: I can write \"Omar and Saad play\" and \"Every player trains.\"","voice":"en-US, Zaki the Falcon narrator voice (bright, friendly)","speed":"0.95"}]},{"id":"GV-L05-U5-LS5","title":"Ask the Coach: Language in Use","objectives":["Students will be able to tell a sports challenge story and give advice.","Students will be able to write an advice-column reply with correct modals and agreement.","I can tell the story of a challenge I managed to overcome.","I can write advice for a young athlete."],"objectives_ar":["سيتمكن الطلاب من سرد قصة تحدٍّ رياضي وتقديم النصيحة.","سيتمكن الطلاب من كتابة رد في عمود نصائح بأفعال ناقصة وتوافق صحيحين.","أستطيع أن أروي قصة تحدٍّ تمكّنت من تجاوزه.","أستطيع أن أكتب نصيحة لرياضي ناشئ."],"vocabulary":[{"word":"athlete","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈæθˌlit/","definition":"a person who is good at sports and trains to compete","arabic":"رياضي","example":"The athlete trains six days a week.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"coach","pos":"noun","ipa":"/koʊtʃ/","definition":"a person who trains a sports team or player","arabic":"مدرّب","example":"Our coach gives us great advice.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"opponent","pos":"noun","ipa":"/əˈpoʊnənt/","definition":"the person or team you play against","arabic":"خصم / منافس","example":"Our opponents were very strong.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"tournament","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈtʊrnəmənt/","definition":"a competition with many games or matches","arabic":"بطولة","example":"Our school won the football tournament.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"stamina","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈstæmənə/","definition":"the energy and strength to keep going for a long time","arabic":"قدرة على التحمّل","example":"Swimming builds your stamina.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"determination","pos":"noun","ipa":"/dɪˌtɝməˈneɪʃən/","definition":"not giving up when something is difficult","arabic":"عزيمة / إصرار","example":"With determination, you can reach your goal.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"injury","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈɪndʒɚi/","definition":"damage to the body, for example in an accident or sport","arabic":"إصابة","example":"He couldn't play because of a knee injury.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"defeat","pos":"noun","ipa":"/dɪˈfit/","definition":"losing a game or a competition","arabic":"هزيمة / خسارة","example":"The team learned a lot from the defeat.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"victory","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈvɪktɚi/","definition":"winning a game or a competition","arabic":"فوز / انتصار","example":"The fans celebrated the victory.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"fitness","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈfɪtnəs/","definition":"being healthy and strong","arabic":"لياقة بدنية","example":"Fitness is important for every athlete.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"teammate","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈtiˌmeɪt/","definition":"a person on the same team as you","arabic":"زميل في الفريق","example":"My teammates passed me the ball.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"spectator","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈspɛkteɪtɚ/","definition":"a person who watches a sports event","arabic":"متفرّج","example":"Thousands of spectators filled the stadium.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"trophy","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈtroʊfi/","definition":"a cup or prize given to the winner","arabic":"كأس / جائزة","example":"The captain lifted the trophy.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"referee","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˌrɛfɚˈi/","definition":"the person who makes sure players follow the rules","arabic":"حَكَم","example":"The referee blew the whistle.","image_id":"","audio_id":""}],"explanation":{"text":"Today you use all of Unit 5: could / was able to / managed to, should / ought to / had better, sports phrasal verbs and agreement with compound subjects.\nAn advice reply shows understanding, shares a past experience (could / managed to), gives advice (should / ought to / had better) and encourages (carry on, don't give up).\n✔ could (general) • was able to / managed to (one success) • will be able to\n✔ should / ought to / 'd better (not) + base verb\n✔ warm up, work out, keep up with, give up, carry on\n✔ A and B + plural • every + singular • team + singular","examples":["When I was younger, I couldn't swim well.","Last summer, I managed to swim across the bay.","You should eat more fruit.","You ought to sleep earlier on school nights.","You'd better see a doctor about that knee.","Don't give up – carry on training!","Your parents and your coach are proud of you.","Every athlete has good and bad days."],"common_errors":["✗ Last week I could win my first match. → ✓ Last week I managed to win my first match. (One success → managed to.)","✗ You should to rest more. → ✓ You should rest more. (should + base verb.)","✗ My coach and my teammates is proud of me. → ✓ My coach and my teammates are proud of me. (A and B → plural.)"],"arabic":"نراجع could وwas able to وmanaged to وshould وought to وhad better والأفعال المركبة الرياضية وتوافق الفعل مع الفاعل المركب.\nالرد في عمود النصائح يُظهر التفهم، ويذكر تجربة ماضية، ويقدّم نصائح، ويشجّع.\nتذكّر: managed to للنجاح بعد صعوبة، وhad better not للتحذير."},"video":{"script":[{"time":"00:00–00:20","narration":"Hi, I’m Zaki! Today’s lesson is “Ask the Coach: Language in Use”. Let’s learn could / was able to / managed to, should / ought to / had better, sports phrasal verbs, subject–verb agreement with compound subjects (unit review) together.","on_screen":"GV-L05-U5-LS5 | Ask the Coach: Language in Use","visual":"Zaki the Falcon flies in over the Leaf Green title card and lands next to the lesson title; soft chime."},{"time":"00:20–00:45","narration":"Let’s use everything from this unit. Your task: Coach's corner role-play: Student A is a young athlete with a problem (losing matches, no stamina, an injury, fear of competing). Student B is the coach: he asks about the past (could you…? were you able to…?) and gives 3 pieces of advice (should, ought to, had better). Then swap roles.","on_screen":"Language in use","visual":"Split screen: two cast members talking, with speech bubbles appearing."},{"time":"00:45–01:30","narration":"Today you use all of Unit 5: could / was able to / managed to, should / ought to / had better, sports phrasal verbs and agreement with compound subjects. An advice reply shows understanding, shares a past experience (could / managed to), gives advice (should / ought to / had better) and encourages (carry on, don't give up).","on_screen":"✔ could (general) • was able to / managed to (one success) • will be able to • ✔ should / ought to / 'd better (not) + base verb • ✔ warm up, work out, keep up with, give up, carry on","visual":"Grammar box builds line by line (form → meaning → use); Zaki points to each line."},{"time":"01:30–02:15","narration":"Here are some examples. When I was younger, I couldn't swim well. Last summer, I managed to swim across the bay. You should eat more fruit.","on_screen":"When I was younger, I couldn't swim well. / Last summer, I managed to swim across the bay. / You should eat more fruit.","visual":"Each example appears on a card with a small picture; the target part bounces once and turns green."},{"time":"02:15–02:45","narration":"Be careful! Some learners say: “Last week I could win my first match.” That’s not right. We say: “Last week I managed to win my first match.” One success → managed to.","on_screen":"✗ Last week I could win my first match.   ✓ Last week I managed to win my first match.","visual":"A red cross stamps the wrong sentence; it slides away and the correct sentence appears with a green tick."},{"time":"02:45–03:05","narration":"Quick check: what is wrong with “You should to rest more.”? … Think! … It should be “You should rest more.”. Great job! Now you can say: I can tell the story of a challenge I managed to overcome.","on_screen":"Quick check ✔  |  I can: I can tell the story of a challenge I managed to overcome.","visual":"Three-second pause with a countdown ring; Zaki gives a wing \"thumbs up\" and confetti in the programme colours."}],"captions_ar":["مرحبًا، أنا زكي! درس اليوم: could / was able to / managed to, should / ought to / had better, sports phrasal verbs, subject–verb agreement with compound subjects (unit review).","استمع وانتبه إلى الكلمات الملوّنة بالأخضر.","نراجع could وwas able to وmanaged to وshould وought to وhad better والأفعال المركبة الرياضية وتوافق الفعل مع الفاعل المركب.","لاحظ الأمثلة: الجزء الملوّن هو القاعدة.","انتبه! الخطأ: Last week I could win my first match. — الصحيح: Last week I managed to win my first match.","سؤال سريع… فكّر ثم أجب. أحسنت! الآن تستطيع: أستطيع أن أروي قصة تحدٍّ تمكّنت من تجاوزه."]},"texts":[{"type":"model","title":"Ask Coach Hamdan","body":"Dear Coach, I'm on the school football team, but I always get tired after twenty minutes. Last week, I couldn't keep up with the other players, and I wanted to give up. What should I do? – Adam\nDear Adam, don't worry! When I was your age, I couldn't run for long either.\nBut I trained every day, and in the end, I managed to play full matches.\nFirst, you should build your stamina. You ought to jog three times a week.\nYou'd better not skip breakfast on match days.\nAlso, warm up properly and cool down after every game.\nYour coach and your teammates believe in you, and every player has bad days.\nCarry on, and you'll be able to play the whole match soon! – Coach Hamdan","word_count":129,"audio_id":"AUD-GV-L05-U5-LS5-T1"}],"activities":[{"id":"GV-L05-U5-LS5-A1","type":"true_false","instructions":"Controlled practice: Read \"Ask Coach Hamdan\". True or False?","items":[{"q":"Adam gets tired after twenty minutes.","options":["True","False"],"answer":"True","explanation":"Check the text “Ask Coach Hamdan”.","explanation_ar":"ارجع إلى النص وابحث عن الجملة التي تعطي الإجابة."},{"q":"Last week, Adam was able to keep up with the other players.","options":["True","False"],"answer":"False","explanation":"Check the text “Ask Coach Hamdan”.","explanation_ar":"ارجع إلى النص وابحث عن الجملة التي تعطي الإجابة."},{"q":"The coach could run for a long time when he was young.","options":["True","False"],"answer":"False","explanation":"Check the text “Ask Coach Hamdan”.","explanation_ar":"ارجع إلى النص وابحث عن الجملة التي تعطي الإجابة."},{"q":"The coach managed to play full matches in the end.","options":["True","False"],"answer":"True","explanation":"Check the text “Ask Coach Hamdan”.","explanation_ar":"ارجع إلى النص وابحث عن الجملة التي تعطي الإجابة."},{"q":"The coach tells Adam to skip breakfast on match days.","options":["True","False"],"answer":"False","explanation":"Check the text “Ask Coach Hamdan”.","explanation_ar":"ارجع إلى النص وابحث عن الجملة التي تعطي الإجابة."},{"q":"The coach says Adam ought to jog every day.","options":["True","False"],"answer":"False","explanation":"Check the text “Ask Coach Hamdan”.","explanation_ar":"ارجع إلى النص وابحث عن الجملة التي تعطي الإجابة."}]},{"id":"GV-L05-U5-LS5-B1","type":"mcq","instructions":"Guided practice: Choose the correct answer.","items":[{"q":"Last week, Adam ___ keep up with the other players.","options":["wasn't able","couldn't","didn't manage","can't to"],"answer":"couldn't","explanation":"could / was able to / managed to / will be able to.","explanation_ar":"القدرة."},{"q":"In the end, the coach ___ play full matches.","options":["managed to","can","could","manage"],"answer":"managed to","explanation":"could / was able to / managed to / will be able to.","explanation_ar":"القدرة."},{"q":"You ___ jog three times a week.","options":["ought to","should to","ought","had better to"],"answer":"ought to","explanation":"should / ought to / had better.","explanation_ar":"النصيحة."},{"q":"You'd ___ skip breakfast on match days.","options":["not better","better to not","better not to","better not"],"answer":"better not","explanation":"should / ought to / had better.","explanation_ar":"النصيحة."},{"q":"Warm up properly and ___ after every game.","options":["cool out","cool off it","cool down","cool up"],"answer":"cool down","explanation":"Sports phrasal verb.","explanation_ar":"الفعل المركب."},{"q":"Your coach and your teammates ___ in you.","options":["is believing","believes","has believed","believe"],"answer":"believe","explanation":"Subject–verb agreement.","explanation_ar":"التوافق."}]},{"id":"GV-L05-U5-LS5-C1","type":"open","instructions":"Freer practice: Prepare your coach's corner role-play.","items":[{"q":"My problem as a young athlete: ___","options":[],"answer":"I always lose my concentration in the second half.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses could / was able to / managed to, should / ought to / had better, sports phrasal verbs, subject–verb agreement with compound subjects (unit review) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"A question the coach asks about the past (could / were you able to): ___","options":[],"answer":"Were you able to concentrate in your last match?","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses could / was able to / managed to, should / ought to / had better, sports phrasal verbs, subject–verb agreement with compound subjects (unit review) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"The coach's first tip with should: ___","options":[],"answer":"You should get more sleep before matches.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses could / was able to / managed to, should / ought to / had better, sports phrasal verbs, subject–verb agreement with compound subjects (unit review) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"The coach's second tip with ought to: ___","options":[],"answer":"You ought to practice breathing exercises.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses could / was able to / managed to, should / ought to / had better, sports phrasal verbs, subject–verb agreement with compound subjects (unit review) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Warning (had better / had better not): ___","options":[],"answer":"You'd better not use your phone late at night.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses could / was able to / managed to, should / ought to / had better, sports phrasal verbs, subject–verb agreement with compound subjects (unit review) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."}]},{"id":"GV-L05-U5-LS5-D1","type":"open","instructions":"Production: Write your reply to a young athlete.","items":[{"q":"Plan: the problem, your past experience (could / managed to), 3 pieces of advice, 2 phrasal verbs, encouragement.","options":[],"answer":"problem: afraid of swimming races; could: I couldn't dive at first; managed to win a medal; advice: practice starts, ought to visualize, 'd better not panic; phrasal: warm up, carry on","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses could / was able to / managed to, should / ought to / had better, sports phrasal verbs, subject–verb agreement with compound subjects (unit review) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write your reply in 7–8 sentences.","options":[],"answer":"Dear Faisal, I understand how you feel. When I was ten, I couldn't dive at all. But I practiced every day, and last year I managed to win a medal. You should practice your starts every day. You ought to imagine a good race before you sleep. You'd better not look at the other swimmers. Warm up well and carry on – your family and your coach are behind you!","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses could / was able to / managed to, should / ought to / had better, sports phrasal verbs, subject–verb agreement with compound subjects (unit review) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Check: modals + base verb? agreement? phrasal verbs? Fix one mistake.","options":[],"answer":"I changed \"Your family and your coach is behind you\" to \"Your family and your coach are behind you\".","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses could / was able to / managed to, should / ought to / had better, sports phrasal verbs, subject–verb agreement with compound subjects (unit review) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."}]}],"game":{"name":"Treasure Hunt Review","mechanic":"timed_quiz","rules":["Teams move to a station every 90 seconds.","At each station, write one correct sentence with the cue word and could / was able to / managed to, should / ought to / had better, sports phrasal verbs, subject–verb agreement with compound subjects (unit review).","Teams collect a \"treasure letter\" for each correct sentence.","Unscramble the letters at the end to reveal the secret word."],"data":[{"cue":"athlete","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"coach","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"opponent","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"tournament","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"stamina","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"determination","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"injury","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"defeat","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"victory","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"fitness","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"teammate","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"spectator","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"trophy","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"referee","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]}]},"exit_quiz":[{"q":"When she was eight, Noura ___ swim 500 meters.","options":["managed","was able","could","can to"],"answer":"could","explanation":"could / was able to / managed to / will be able to.","explanation_ar":"القدرة."},{"q":"","options":["After three tries, Faisal could lift the weight yesterday.","After three tries, Faisal was able to lift the weight.","After three tries, Faisal was able lift the weight.","After three tries, Faisal able to lift the weight."],"answer":"After three tries, Faisal was able to lift the weight.","explanation":"could / was able to / managed to / will be able to.","explanation_ar":"القدرة."},{"q":"You've hurt your wrist. You ___ play volleyball today.","options":["'d not better","hadn't better to","'d better not","better not to"],"answer":"'d better not","explanation":"should / ought to / had better.","explanation_ar":"النصيحة."},{"q":"New players ___ listen carefully to the coach.","options":["had better to","ought","should to","should"],"answer":"should","explanation":"should / ought to / had better.","explanation_ar":"النصيحة."},{"q":"I want to ___ a new sport this year – maybe fencing.","options":["cool down","take up","knock out","give up"],"answer":"take up","explanation":"Sports phrasal verb.","explanation_ar":"الفعل المركب."},{"q":"We were losing 2–0, but we didn't ___.","options":["warm up","take up","give up","sign up"],"answer":"give up","explanation":"Sports phrasal verb.","explanation_ar":"الفعل المركب."},{"q":"","options":["Neither Omar nor his brothers want to pull out of the race.","Neither Omar nor his brothers wanting to pull out of the race.","Neither Omar nor his brothers wants to pull out of the race.","Neither Omar nor his brothers is wanting to pull out of the race."],"answer":"Neither Omar nor his brothers want to pull out of the race.","explanation":"Subject–verb agreement.","explanation_ar":"التوافق."},{"q":"","options":["Every teammate are important.","Every teammates is important.","Every teammate be important.","Every teammate is important."],"answer":"Every teammate is important.","explanation":"Subject–verb agreement.","explanation_ar":"التوافق."},{"q":"With more training, you ___ run faster next season.","options":["can to","could to","will can","will be able to"],"answer":"will be able to","explanation":"could / was able to / managed to / will be able to.","explanation_ar":"القدرة."},{"q":"Pick the best sentence for an advice reply:","options":["You should warm up before every match, and you'd better not skip practice.","You should warm before every match up, and you better not to skip practice.","You should to warm up before every match, and you'd better not skip practice.","You should warm up before every match, and you'd not better skip practice."],"answer":"You should warm up before every match, and you'd better not skip practice.","explanation":"should / ought to / had better.","explanation_ar":"النصيحة."}],"images":[{"id":"GV-L05-U5-LS5-IMG01","prompt":"The school magazine page \"Ask Coach Hamdan\" with a letter from a worried student and the coach's reply, a photo of a smiling coach with a whistle, and a small trophy. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as young teenagers aged 12–14, slightly more detailed, energetic composition. Landscape 16:9.","alt":"The school magazine page \"Ask Coach Hamdan\" with a letter from a worried student and the coach's reply, a photo of a smiling coach with a whistle, and a small trophy"},{"id":"GV-L05-U5-LS5-IMG02","prompt":"two programme characters doing the speaking task about “Sport and Challenge”, speech bubbles left empty. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as young teenagers aged 12–14, slightly more detailed, energetic composition. Square 1:1.","alt":"two programme characters doing the speaking task about “Sport and Challenge”, speech bubbles left empty"},{"id":"GV-L05-U5-LS5-G01","prompt":"Card-back and board design for the classroom game “Treasure Hunt Review”: Zaki the Falcon icon in the centre, Leaf Green border with navy stars. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image. Portrait 2:3.","alt":"Game card design for Treasure Hunt Review"}],"audio":[{"id":"AUD-GV-L05-U5-LS5-T1","script":"Dear Coach, I'm on the school football team, but I always get tired after twenty minutes. Last week, I couldn't keep up with the other players, and I wanted to give up. What should I do? – Adam Dear Adam, don't worry! When I was your age, I couldn't run for long either. But I trained every day, and in the end, I managed to play full matches. First, you should build your stamina. You ought to jog three times a week. You'd better not skip breakfast on match days. Also, warm up properly and cool down after every game. Your coach and your teammates believe in you, and every player has bad days. Carry on, and you'll be able to play the whole match soon! – Coach Hamdan","voice":"en-US, friendly young-adult voice","speed":"0.95"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L05-U5-LS5-VID","script":"Hi, I’m Zaki! Today’s lesson is “Ask the Coach: Language in Use”. Let’s learn could / was able to / managed to, should / ought to / had better, sports phrasal verbs, subject–verb agreement with compound subjects (unit review) together. Let’s use everything from this unit. Your task: Coach's corner role-play: Student A is a young athlete with a problem (losing matches, no stamina, an injury, fear of competing). Student B is the coach: he asks about the past (could you…? were you able to…?) and gives 3 pieces of advice (should, ought to, had better). Then swap roles. Today you use all of Unit 5: could / was able to / managed to, should / ought to / had better, sports phrasal verbs and agreement with compound subjects. An advice reply shows understanding, shares a past experience (could / managed to), gives advice (should / ought to / had better) and encourages (carry on, don't give up). Here are some examples. When I was younger, I couldn't swim well. Last summer, I managed to swim across the bay. You should eat more fruit. Be careful! Some learners say: “Last week I could win my first match.” That’s not right. We say: “Last week I managed to win my first match.” One success → managed to. Quick check: what is wrong with “You should to rest more.”? … Think! … It should be “You should rest more.”. Great job! Now you can say: I can tell the story of a challenge I managed to overcome.","voice":"en-US, Zaki the Falcon narrator voice (bright, friendly)","speed":"0.95"}]}],"unit_test":[{"q":"My grandfather ___ swim across the river when he was young.","options":["can to","was able","could","managed"],"answer":"could","explanation":"General past ability → could + base verb.","explanation_ar":"القدرة العامة ← could."},{"q":"The exam was hard, but I ___ pass it.","options":["manage","managed to","can","could"],"answer":"managed to","explanation":"Success after difficulty → managed to + base verb.","explanation_ar":"النجاح بعد صعوبة ← managed to."},{"q":"","options":["The climbers able to reach the top before the storm.","The climbers were able reach the top before the storm.","The climbers could reach the top before the storm yesterday.","The climbers were able to reach the top before the storm."],"answer":"The climbers were able to reach the top before the storm.","explanation":"One specific success → was/were able to.","explanation_ar":"النجاح في موقف محدد ← was able to."},{"q":"I ___ find my keys this morning, so I was late.","options":["can't to","wasn't able","couldn't","didn't manage"],"answer":"couldn't","explanation":"Negative ability → couldn't.","explanation_ar":"النفي ← couldn't."},{"q":"After this course, you ___ play the guitar.","options":["can to","will can","could to","will be able to"],"answer":"will be able to","explanation":"Future ability → will be able to.","explanation_ar":"المستقبل ← will be able to."},{"q":"You have a fever. You ___ stay in bed.","options":["should","should to","had better to","ought"],"answer":"should","explanation":"Advice → should / shouldn't + base verb.","explanation_ar":"النصيحة ← should."},{"q":"Students ___ to respect their teachers.","options":["should","ought","must to","had better"],"answer":"ought","explanation":"ought to + base verb (same as should).","explanation_ar":"ought to."},{"q":"It's going to rain. You ___ take an umbrella.","options":["should to","'d better to","ought","'d better"],"answer":"'d better","explanation":"Strong advice / warning → had better + base verb.","explanation_ar":"التحذير ← had better."},{"q":"","options":["You hadn't better miss the last bus.","You'd better not miss the last bus.","You'd not better miss the last bus.","You'd better not to miss the last bus."],"answer":"You'd better not miss the last bus.","explanation":"Negative warning → had better not.","explanation_ar":"had better not."},{"q":"___ I join the chess club or the robotics club?","options":["Better","Should","Had","Ought"],"answer":"Should","explanation":"Asking for advice → Should I…?","explanation_ar":"طلب النصيحة."},{"q":"Before you run, you should ___ for ten minutes.","options":["work out","give up","warm up","knock out"],"answer":"warm up","explanation":"Choose the phrasal verb with the right meaning.","explanation_ar":"المعنى الصحيح."},{"q":"I'm too tired to ___ the others. They're too fast!","options":["keep out of","keep up with","keep in with","keep on with"],"answer":"keep up with","explanation":"Three-part verbs keep together (keep up with, sign up for, pull out of).","explanation_ar":"الأفعال الثلاثية لا تنفصل."},{"q":"Rakan had to ___ the final because of his injury.","options":["pull up with","pull in of","pull off for","pull out of"],"answer":"pull out of","explanation":"Three-part verbs keep together (keep up with, sign up for, pull out of).","explanation_ar":"الأفعال الثلاثية لا تنفصل."},{"q":"","options":["Swimming is fun. Why do you want to give it up?","Swimming is fun. Why do you want to give up it?","Swimming is fun. Why do you want to up give it?","Swimming is fun. Why do you want to give it out?"],"answer":"Swimming is fun. Why do you want to give it up?","explanation":"Object pronoun goes in the middle (give it up).","explanation_ar":"الضمير في الوسط."},{"q":"The fans ___ the players ___ until the final whistle.","options":["cheered / off","cheered / up","cheered / on","cheered / out"],"answer":"cheered / on","explanation":"Choose the phrasal verb with the right meaning.","explanation_ar":"المعنى الصحيح."},{"q":"My brother and I ___ basketball every Thursday.","options":["plays","has played","play","is playing"],"answer":"play","explanation":"A and B → plural verb.","explanation_ar":"and ← فعل جمع."},{"q":"Every player ___ his own locker.","options":["has","have","are having","having"],"answer":"has","explanation":"each / every → singular verb.","explanation_ar":"each/every ← مفرد."},{"q":"Neither the coach nor the players ___ the news yet.","options":["is hearing","hears","has heard","have heard"],"answer":"have heard","explanation":"or / nor → verb agrees with the nearer subject.","explanation_ar":"or/nor ← الفاعل الأقرب."},{"q":"Our class ___ the best results in the school.","options":["have","has","having","are having"],"answer":"has","explanation":"team / family / class → singular verb (American English).","explanation_ar":"أسماء الجمع ← مفرد."},{"q":"The referee, along with his assistants, ___ walking onto the field.","options":["have been","are","is","were"],"answer":"is","explanation":"along with / as well as → verb agrees with the first subject.","explanation_ar":"along with ← الفاعل الأول."},{"q":"Either my uncles or my father ___ us to the stadium every week.","options":["takes","take","are taking","have taken"],"answer":"takes","explanation":"or / nor → verb agrees with the nearer subject.","explanation_ar":"or/nor ← الفاعل الأقرب."},{"q":"Choose the best sentence for a sports story.","options":["Despite the injury, Lina managed to finish the race, and her family and friends were very proud.","Despite the injury, Lina managed finish the race, and her family and friends were very proud.","Despite the injury, Lina was able finish the race, and her family and friends is proud.","Despite the injury, Lina could finish the race yesterday, and her family and friends was very proud."],"answer":"Despite the injury, Lina managed to finish the race, and her family and friends were very proud.","explanation":"Success after difficulty → managed to + base verb.","explanation_ar":"النجاح بعد صعوبة ← managed to."},{"q":"How can we fix it? \"every athlete should to warm up.\"","options":["every athlete should warm up.","Every athletes should warm up.","Every athlete should to warm up.","Every athlete should warm up."],"answer":"Every athlete should warm up.","explanation":"Advice → should / shouldn't + base verb.","explanation_ar":"النصيحة ← should."},{"q":"Were you able to finish the marathon? – ___","options":["Yes, I managed to finish it in four hours.","Yes, I managed finish it in four hours.","Yes, I could finish it yesterday in four hours.","Yes, I was able finish it in four hours."],"answer":"Yes, I managed to finish it in four hours.","explanation":"Success after difficulty → managed to + base verb.","explanation_ar":"النجاح بعد صعوبة ← managed to."}],"theme_ar":"الرياضة والتحدّي","essential_question_ar":"ما الذي يساعدنا على الاستمرار عندما تصبح الأمور صعبة؟","can_do":"I can talk about past ability and success with could, was able to and managed to, give advice with should, ought to and had better, use sports phrasal verbs, and make verbs agree with compound subjects.","can_do_ar":"أستطيع أن أتحدث عن القدرة والنجاح في الماضي بـ could وwas able to وmanaged to، وأقدّم النصيحة بـ should وought to وhad better، وأستخدم أفعالًا مركبة رياضية، وأجعل الفعل يتوافق مع الفاعل المركب."});
